WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:07.980
- All right, my name is Rod Ferguson

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.340
and I'm the chair of Women's,
Gender, and Sexuality Studies.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.350
It's my pleasure to welcome
you to our 2022 Brudner Event.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.110
This is a very special moment for us

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:19.810
because this is the first
time that we've had it

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:20.853
since the lockdown.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.550
The Brudner Prize established
in 2000, is awarded annually

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.460
to an accomplished scholar,
artist, or activist

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.890
whose work has made
significant contributions

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.020
to LGBT studies and LGBT communities.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.570
All right, we have
decided to pluralize that

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:39.970
this evening with the panel.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.400
And tonight's panelists
certainly fit that description.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.020
Our event is named after James Brudner

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.050
who was an AIDS activist, urban planner,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.330
journalist, and photographer.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.220
A man of wit and compassion,

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.740
outsize knowledge and curiosity,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:00.930
Brudner valued both academic
inquiry and direct action.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.500
He spent 12 years as a policy analyst

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.100
for the city of New York.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.730
He also earned an MA in journalism
from New York University,

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.980
and wrote for various publications

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.770
on gay and AIDS-related topics.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.400
He became a member of ACT UP,
the Treatment Action Group,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.010
and other organizations after
the death of his twin brother,

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.970
Eric of AIDS in 1987.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.510
He worked on treatment
and prevention issues

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.690
with the National Institutes of Health,

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.100
pharmaceutical corporations,
and federal agencies.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.060
In his final years, he
devoted much of his time

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.880
to traveling the back roads of
rural America with a camera.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.860
La MaMa Galleria in New
York mounted an exhibition

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.970
of his photographs in 1997.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:48.190
He died of AIDS-related
illness on September 18th, 1998

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.370
at the age of 37.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:55.250
Through his will, he established
the Brudner Prize at Yale

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.070
as a quote, perpetual
annual prize, end quote,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.850
for scholarship and activism
on gay and lesbian history

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.310
and contemporary experience.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.450
Our thanks go first to
the Brudner Executors,

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.130
Steven Carlin, Michael
Cormier and Ben Pesner.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:16.260
Also, we'd like to thank the WGSS staff,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.400
Ellen Cupo, Moe Gardner,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.860
Keith Geriak and Linda Relyea

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.290
for the support that they offered.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.850
Our thanks also to Rick Leone
from Yale Broadcast Center

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.700
for AV support.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.780
Our thanks also go to
the LGBT Studies committee

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.220
made up of Jill Richards,
the committee chair,

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.330
Joe Fischel, Scott
Herring and Greta LaFleur.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.640
Particularly, we want to thank Joe

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.310
for organizing tonight's panel.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.657
Joe, who is the author of "Screw Consent:

00:02:49.657 --> 00:02:51.750
"A Better Politics of Sexual Justice"

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.420
and "Sex and Harm in the Age of Consent"

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.850
will introduce the panelists.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.031
Please welcome Joe Fischel.

00:03:00.031 --> 00:03:03.324
(audience applauding)

00:03:03.324 --> 00:03:04.379
- I'll have you go first.

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:06.796
(Joe laughs)

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.850
- It's better that way.
- Yeah.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.433
This is better, this is better.

00:03:18.382 --> 00:03:19.215
Okay.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.220
Hi, everyone and thank you, Rod.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.350
Great, so yeah, thank you.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.060
Welcome to the Brudner.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.490
I'm gonna keep my comments short

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.280
and then I will introduce the speakers

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.970
and the introductions will also be short.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.120
I apologize that I'm gonna
lift my introductions

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.390
right from the program
that you are holding,

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.310
which we lifted from their websites.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:44.253
But the point of keeping it short

00:03:44.253 --> 00:03:47.313
is that you can hear them and not me.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.760
So the question I will
soon pose to our panelists

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:57.320
is anchored by two, or we
could think it of as anchored

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:00.140
by two geopolitical phenomena.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.233
The first, Russia's war against Ukraine.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.340
And even the most materialist

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:10.520
intersectional feminist
scholar wonders sometimes,

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.890
isn't all of that sex and
gender stuff superfluous

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:18.123
or epiphenomenal to the
brutality and violence of war?

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.747
Of course, many scholars and
some scholars in this room

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:28.440
have written about the
implication of war making,

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.440
war legitimation and insistent normativity

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.520
around sex and gender.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.840
Many have pointed out how
Russian officials and pundits

00:04:39.333 --> 00:04:43.400
have drawn on gayness,
gender non-normativity

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.970
as threats to Russia,
Russian civilization,

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:49.260
and children in particular,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.620
to authorize Russian aggression

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.233
and its aggregation of power.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:00.173
So the second anchor point,
maybe a surprise to nobody.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.760
We could take anxiety generating stock

00:05:06.347 --> 00:05:10.900
of the legislative attack
on girls and women,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.020
queer kids and trans kids,

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.800
an attack ever more vicious and lethal

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.460
against girls, women, and queer
and trans kids minoritized

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.380
along other axes of inequality,

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.953
race, class, citizenship status.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.507
If commentators note
the rhetorics of gender

00:05:27.507 --> 00:05:29.110
and sexual normativity

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:32.930
that scaffold Russian authoritarianism,

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.410
we might observe how anti-gay, anti-trans,

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:39.640
and anti-women legislation rests

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:43.520
extraordinary regulatory
surveillance and punitive power

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.323
into the hands of our legislators.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.950
So a thought behind the
question, what is sexual justice

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:54.330
is how to think those phenomena
and those dangers together,

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:57.200
even as the scale of our scholars' answers

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.713
will travel from the couple
form to the planetary.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.633
So that's kind of all I've
got by way of scaffolding.

00:06:05.756 --> 00:06:07.150
I'm gonna introduce our speakers

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.963
and kind of more formally
ask them this question.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.900
So, if the font were bigger.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.350
Okay, so Paisley Currah is a professor

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:17.980
of Political Science and
Women's &amp; Gender Studies

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:21.430
at Brooklyn College and the
Graduate Center of CUNY.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.940
Professor Currah has written
widely on transgender issues,

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.900
including on topics such as discrimination

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.560
and sex reclassification,

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:29.600
and the transgender rights movement.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.280
He is the author and editor
of over 30 articles and books

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.190
and co-founder of the leading journal

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:36.027
in transgender studies, "TSQ:

00:06:36.027 --> 00:06:38.040
"Transgender Studies Quarterly".

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.817
Currah's newest book, sex
is, there's a typo here,

00:06:39.817 --> 00:06:42.740
"Sex Is as Sex Does: Governing
Transgender Identity"

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:47.390
will be released in the
spring of 2022 by NYU Press.

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:49.150
I should say that my lecture

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.310
in gender justice power institutions

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:51.470
have the extraordinary opportunity

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.660
to read some of Professor Currah's book

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:57.500
and also texts of our
other panelists today,

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.763
which has been fantastic.

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:03.080
Che Gossett is the racial
justice postdoctoral fellow

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:05.270
in initiative for a just society

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:07.180
at the Center for
Contemporary Critical Thought

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:08.890
at Columbia university.

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.810
Che Gossett is a black,
non-binary femme writer.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:12.870
They'll be a visiting scholar

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.070
at the Center for Life-Writing
at Wolfson College,

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:18.470
Oxford University in 2022/23.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:21.310
Gossett was a 2019/2020
Helena Rubinstein Fellow

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:22.500
in Critical Studies,

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.390
in the Whitney Independent Study Program

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.070
as well as a 2020/2021 graduate fellow

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.970
at the Center for Cultural
Analysis at Rutgers University.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:32.980
They also co-edited with
professor David Getsy.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:34.020
I love David Getsy.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.030
They co-edited with Professor David Getsy

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.890
a syllabus on trans and non-binary methods

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:41.190
for Art Journal which
just recently received

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.663
the 2022 CAA Journal Award.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.380
Tey Meadow, associate
professor of Sociology

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:48.960
at Columbia University.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.100
Tenured like last week, so yay.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:54.267
(audience applauding)

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.120
Professor Meadow is associate
professor of sociology

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.710
