00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:14.590
as well as numerous scholarly titles.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.780
She is currently completing
a new ethnographic book,

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.547
tentatively titled "Edge Play:

00:08:18.547 --> 00:08:20.877
"The Erotic Life of Social Difference".

00:08:22.761 --> 00:08:23.630
Shatema Threadcraft,

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:26.400
associate professor of
Gender and Sexuality Studies,

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.970
Philosophy, and Political
Science at Vanderbilt University.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:32.147
Professor Threadcraft is the
author of "Intimate Justice:

00:08:32.147 --> 00:08:34.580
"The black Female Body
and the Body Politic."

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.413
which was the winner

00:08:35.413 --> 00:08:37.630
of the National Women's
Studies Association

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.760
Sara H Whaley Award for the
Best Book on Women and Labor.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.720
The 2017 W. E. B. Du Bois
Distinguished Book Award

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.080
from the National Conference
of black Political Scientists.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.580
And finally, the 2017 Best Book Award

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:50.950
for the American Political
Science Association's Race,

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:53.400
Ethnicity, and Politics Organized Section.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.270
It's an awesome book.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:57.340
So those are my introductions.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.363
And I will leave with the question,

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:02.460
what is sexual justice?

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:05.480
What does sexual justice
demand institutionally,

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:08.970
politically, ethically, and aesthetically?

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:10.660
How do we envision sexual justice

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.510
within and across activisms and intimacies

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:16.740
inside and beside pandemic life?

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:21.740
So we will start with Che
Gossett and go down the row.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:24.650
Also, there's lots of
like snacks and coffee,

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.960
so make sure you get that
at the end of the event.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:28.710
Okay, I'll turn it over to you.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.101
Cool.

00:09:30.101 --> 00:09:33.268
(audience applauding)

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:46.370
- Thank you so much, Joe and to Rod

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:51.930
and to the amazing co
Brudner roundtable panelists.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:54.403
It's such an honor to be here.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.630
And especially someone who's
been reading your individual

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.410
and collective work for many years,

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:06.200
I appreciate this invitation
to think through this question

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.143
of sexual justice.

00:10:10.396 --> 00:10:15.396
So I'm just gonna be reading

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:20.390
and thinking through sexual
justice through the aesthetic,

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:22.770
through art, through black trans art

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:24.030
in the afterlife of slavery,

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:29.030
and hopefully during the Q and A,

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.760
can talk through some
of the cardinal points

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.420
or orientation points
that you brought up, Joe,

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:37.563
that kinda anchor this the conversation.

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:43.863
So I want to open with a
quote by Hortense Spillers.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.240
In order to name the
black woman in the sexual,

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.470
the investigator is obliged
to back all the way up

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:55.730
to that suspenseful chapter in
the unfolding of subjecthood

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:59.920
which begins for Africanity
in the West, not with a body,

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.270
which one sees well enough,

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:06.280
but with which that body was made to mean,

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.250
with what that body was made to mean

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.233
via the powerful grammars of capture.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.270
Building on black feminist
and literature professor,

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:17.100
Hortense Spillers's
theoretical articulation

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.870
of grammars of capture,

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.890
my work considers how great
these grammars function

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:25.040
as the political vernacular
through which carceral regimes

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:26.763
are both rehearsed and renewed.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:32.570
I examine aesthetic objects and
cinematic interventions made

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:35.790
by black radical queer and
trans performers and artists

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:40.420
and writers and how these
interventions perform

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:43.700
a deconstruction of what she
calls the grammars of capture

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.340
and the carceral
metaphysics of the subject

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:48.890
by marshaling fugitive forms of aesthetics

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:51.320
and untraditional archives.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.680
So in this brief talk or segment,

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.270
I think through the work of my sibling,

00:11:57.270 --> 00:12:00.820
the black trans artist, Tourmaline,

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:04.170
and how she engages in
what Saidiya Hartman terms,

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:05.120
critical fabulation

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.380
in resistance to the
aesthetic and racial regime

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:11.783
of the archive of slavery,

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:15.260
at the same time in
resistance to the normative

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:17.500
and neoliberal politics of what today

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:21.493
we call transvisibility in
our contemporary moment.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:27.330
Black trans historical
figures are fugitive

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.510
to the terms of the official archive,

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:32.140
except in moments of criminalization

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:34.700
when they appear through
register of the police record

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:36.550
or the court transcript.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:40.740
Which is where the 1836
case of Mary Jones enters

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.733
into the realm of historical
legibility and visibility.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:47.740
I first encountered the
historical figure of Mary Jones

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.320
in Tavia Nyong'o who's here,

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:54.477
who does amazing work in his
text, "Amalgamation Waltz."

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:57.240
And like any proper archivist,

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.910
I immediately rushed to
order the court transcript.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:02.170
Jones was arrested in 1836.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:05.610
She was a sex worker in New York City.

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:08.860
She was brought into court,

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:12.930
sensationalized through like
a lithograph that circulated,

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:16.520
went viral for this
early age of print media.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:21.440
And also was interrogated for
her gender in the courtroom

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:25.830
and it was this spectacle
and political theater.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:28.277
And so in the courtroom, she's asked,

00:13:28.277 --> 00:13:30.760
"Who taught you to dress this way?"

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:33.647
And she replies defiantly,

00:13:33.647 --> 00:13:34.967
"I've always dressed this way

00:13:34.967 --> 00:13:37.447
"amongst people of my own color."

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:41.970
And so that always is really
profound in terms of thinking

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.170
about black trans temporalities

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:46.293
and existence and resistance.

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:53.910
So in "Salacia", Tourmaline's
film about Mary Jones,

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:56.000
which is an aesthetic mediation

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.300
and critical fabulation of her biography,

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.120
Jones is played by actor Rowin Amone

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:02.293
who's pictured here.

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:06.530
Tourmaline's work moves
in the liminal space

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:08.870
between the historical
record of black trans life,

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.270
as well as the realm of the magical.

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:13.500
It is black, queer, and
trans speculation part

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.590
of an aesthetic legacy and genealogy

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:17.630
in tradition of Octavia Butler,

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:21.423
who Rod is gracing our
presence within his T-shirt,

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:26.290
Chip Delany and also the
myriad of such films,

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:28.330
insurgent aesthetics of such films

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:31.950
as "Born in Flames" that
guides the optics of "Salacia."

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.770
The short film takes
place in Seneca village,

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:36.620
a punitively free black enclave

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:38.260
that was eventually destroyed,

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:41.480
showing the rues of
black property ownership,

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:43.800
property in self or of objects,

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:47.070
and it was raised to erect
what is now Central Park,

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:49.577
and that happened in the 1850s.

00:14:49.577 --> 00:14:51.760
"Salacia" dramatizes
the intramural tensions

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.200
that cut across and through
the black community.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:56.420
A community rendered
always already under duress

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.650
by slave catchers in the North

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.270
as well as what Frank Wilkinson calls

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:03.683
the emergency of civil society itself.

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:08.010
A black abolitionist, Peter
Dermot runs both a free press

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:09.240
and a boarding house

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:11.400
and watches with this day out the window

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:14.860
as Jones engages in illicit sex work

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.560
and trade with white men outside.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:19.890
The intramural then is
not without its fractures

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:20.923
and fissions.

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:25.660
Men monster posters surround the city,

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:29.400
which actually happened
in the historical record.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.160
And these lithographs are
pasted walls and trees

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:34.550
signifying that Jones is wanted.

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:37.610
The entire community
is also subject always

00:15:37.610 --> 00:15:39.210
to imminent capture.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:41.510
Jones's situation there
is both the collective

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:44.450
and intermural one, but also
the particular predicament

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:45.873
of black trans fugitivity.

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:50.230
Dermot disapproves of
Jones immorality and vice,

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:51.733
but still provides her refuge.

00:15:53.476 --> 00:15:54.870
In the version of the film installed

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:55.703
at the Brooklyn Museum,

00:15:55.703 --> 00:15:59.290
which was this amazing kind
of 50th anniversary exhibition

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:02.960
commemorating Stonewall,

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:05.340
titled "Nobody Promised You Tomorrow"

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:08.063
which was a Marsha P. Johnson quote,

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:14.280
we see the glimpse of
a glimmer of an affair.

00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:17.500
While Dermot judges Jones

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.770
for her cardinal sins of the flesh,

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:22.783
he is not protected by respectability.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:28.123
The police storm his home
looking for Jones showing

00:16:28.123 --> 00:16:30.790
that black life is never safe or protected

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:32.910
and that autonomy is always
equality of whiteness

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:34.673
as bodily and communal sovereignty.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:39.160
They threatened to close down
his entire establishment.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:40.640
Jones goes on the run.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:44.020
Fugitive, she is cornered,
trapped, resists, bites,

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:46.210
but is overpowered and arrested.

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:47.920
She knows magic however.

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:50.480
And this is part of a kind of ode to

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:55.480
or kind of enactment of black cosmological

00:16:56.880 --> 00:16:58.810
and African indigenous traditions.

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:03.810
So she uses magic to time
travel to the present,

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:07.120
which is also part of a
black speculative imaginary

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:10.220
of 19th century fiction
and slave narratives

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:12.070
like Freddie Douglass' narrative,

00:17:12.070 --> 00:17:16.060
where he gets a magical
amulet from his friend, Sandy,

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:18.220
that he says is part of the,

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:22.950
kind of gave him power to
overthrow Master Covey.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.480
So in this film, Jones
uses water as a transport

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:27.900
and moves through in
between time allowing her

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.113
to escape the confines of her jail cell.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:36.580
And in doing so, she
violates the laws of physics

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:38.810
and moves queerly or what Tavia Nyong'o

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:41.700
might call in an angular fashion

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:43.610
across the entanglement of space time.

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:45.490
So in some ways the film is about

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:47.663
what Daphne Brooks calls escapology.

00:17:51.001 --> 00:17:54.020
She manages to escape
one version of capture,

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:57.577
the pre-emancipation past
only to land in another,

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.953
which would be the anti-black
present of Central Park.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:06.920
Tourmaline's film asked
us to consider the ways

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.240
in which anti-black and
anti-trans temporalities

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.960
are insufficient for black trans freedom

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.483
and how the world remains
a grammar of capture.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:16.810
The film raises questions

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:19.410
about sexual justice and fugitivity,

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.260
and the condition of black trans existence

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:23.753
beyond the racialized
and anti-trans world.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:27.373
And so I wanna end with some questions.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:30.440
What might happen in the
realm of the aesthetic,

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.130
which is disallowed and foreclosed

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:33.630
in the realm of the political?

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.770
Where does the aesthetic
begin and the political end?

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:39.840
Black literary and aesthetic practices

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:42.290
have historically been a spear in an arena

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:44.900
where the political and its
representative functions,

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:47.080
racial liberalism slash democracy,

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:49.800
are left aside for
alternative imaginaries.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:51.570
Or where the strict boundaries

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.810
between the two dissolve
in their interplay.

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:56.410
So thinking about Toni
Morrison's "Beloved"

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:59.600
to Sutton Griggs' fictional imagining

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:01.373
of a sovereign black state.

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:05.530
So in both fiction and nonfiction,

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:09.030
these instead become areas or genres

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:12.750
where political experimentation happens

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:14.683
and where justice to come is staged.

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:17.690
And in "Salacia," the
philosophical edifice

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:19.970
of justice emerges from a vantage point

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:23.460
of the runaway slave, the
radical ethics of the slave

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:27.630
that aims for what Moton
and Harney emphatically say,

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:30.060
charge or demand the
abolition of a society

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:34.820
that could even have prisons itself.

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:36.880
And so therefore,
abolition is an unfinished

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:39.760
and open invitation to
an emancipatory project

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:44.753
that's both interior and
exterior perennial as well.

00:19:44.753 --> 00:19:47.403
And so I think, I guess just to close,

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:51.970
I feel curious about the political grammar

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:55.170
of sexual justice given that justice

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:58.980
is often part of a
grammar of legal capture

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.500
and incrementable justice and the law

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:05.523
are often collapsed into
each other and conflated.

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:11.670
But of course, from
Walter Benjamin to Derrida

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:15.040
to Martin Luther King
Jr., we learn all about,

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:18.040
there's a whole wealth of kind of study

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:20.190
that thinks about the incrementability

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:21.590
of justice and the law.

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:26.520
And so I wonder about the
grammar of sexual justice

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:29.800
and perhaps maybe if there might be ways

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:32.330
to imagine a new political grammar

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.900
beyond the idea of justice itself,

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:37.240
which kind of carries a baggage

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:42.240
that's all about like
reconciliation, restitution,

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:47.220
two sides that come into
kind of a cosmic balance.

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:50.790
And so I guess I have that
left as an open question

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:52.850
for us to all consider together.

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:54.026
Thank you.

00:20:54.026 --> 00:20:57.193
(audience applauding)

00:21:01.854 --> 00:21:03.437
- Excuse me.
- Okay.

00:21:06.933 --> 00:21:07.992
- Okay.

00:21:07.992 --> 00:21:11.325
(indistinct whispering)

00:21:20.163 --> 00:21:20.996
- Okay.

00:21:31.862 --> 00:21:33.639
- Can you see?
- I can, I can.

00:21:33.639 --> 00:21:35.950
I think I can, so this will
move it back and forth.

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:36.783
Okay, thank you.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:40.880
Okay.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:42.330
Can you hear me?

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:43.310
Okay.

00:21:43.310 --> 00:21:48.310
So I wanna thank Professor
Fischel for the invitation.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:52.463
It's great to be here and
among such great scholars.

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:55.770
What is sexual justice?

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:59.370
So since I wrote my
book, "Intimate Justice"

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:02.700
and the "North American
Necropolitics and Gender"

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:05.130
that Professor Fischel's students read,

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:07.360
I've learned a great deal
from black feminists,

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:09.900
sovereign queer and trans abolitionists,

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:12.370
namely from Beth Richie, Dana Melion,

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:14.870
Nick Estes, and Melanie Yazzie.

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:16.410
Cathy Cohen, Eric Stanley,

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.660
Dean Spade, Mariame
Kaba, and Andrea Ritchie.

00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:22.770
So I've learned about the role of police,

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:25.120
not only in enforcing gender normativity,

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:27.060
policing gender and gender expression,

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:30.040
but also in the disproportionate
interpersonal violence

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:32.070
that black women experience.

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:34.510
So my book and the article, I think,

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:36.980
focused on intimate interpersonal violence

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:40.330
and really on that, as
opposed to state violence

00:22:40.330 --> 00:22:42.940
and these authors as well
as activist formations,

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:46.010
including Insight and
Kaba's Survived and Punished

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:47.900
have helped me to see
more clearly something

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:50.080
like intimate state by violence.

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:52.780
So that includes the role
of police and policing

00:22:52.780 --> 00:22:55.760
as well as a broader
tough on crime approach

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:58.160
in perpetuating violence against women,

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:03.000
particularly black women
who violate gender norms

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:04.730
though black women violate gender norms

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:07.010
by their existence in some sense.

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:11.410
Now, but also not only in
perpetuating the violence,

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:13.700
but in escalating the
interpersonal violence,

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:16.040
gender-based violence
that they experience.

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:19.500
Now, my current research
looks at the politics

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:21.810
of black death and how black politics

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:24.470
from lynching to the
movement for black lives

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:26.840
has centered spectacular death.

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:28.780
This presents a problem for black women

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:32.276
who are less likely to be
killed in the spectacular ways

00:23:32.276 --> 00:23:34.070
that CIS black men are.

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:36.430
Men are more likely to be
killed in public generally

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:38.590
by acquaintances and strangers,

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:41.860
and in the era inaugurated
by the Rodney King beating

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:43.630
more likely to have the violence and death

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:46.350
that they experience
filmed and circulated.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:47.950
Women on the other hand are more likely

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:50.330
to be killed in private by intimates

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:52.070
and because of intimacy.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:54.790
This is often even the
case when black women

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:56.190
are killed by police.

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:58.550
So Andrea Ritchie states
that police brutality

00:23:58.550 --> 00:24:01.480
against black women takes
place inside the home,

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:04.180
and it is often unseen as a result.

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:07.240
So here, Breonna Taylor's death echoes

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:09.080
the deaths of many Black women whose names

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:10.880
are now called upon to say,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.950
including Eleanor Bumpurs,
Aiyana Stanley-Jones,

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:16.600
Atatiana Jefferson, Kathryn Johnston,

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:19.240
Shirleen Alaos, and Pearlie Golden.

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:21.580
So much of the violence
takes place inside the home,

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:25.670
in welfare offices, patrol
cars, or even in precincts,

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:28.850
as women attempt to report
intimate partner violence.

00:24:28.850 --> 00:24:30.790
A significant portion
of the violence happens

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:33.370
when black and other
marginalized women ask for help

00:24:33.370 --> 00:24:35.400
in their intimate relationships.

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:37.240
So on the violence and death that occurs

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:39.060
at the intersection of the war on drugs

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:40.680
and the sphere of intimate relations

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:42.570
as was the case with Taylor.

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:45.150
So a warrant was issued
for Taylor's arrest

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:47.100
because of her association,

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:49.320
her intimacy with her former partner.

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.030
And she's now dead.

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:53.210
Here, no knock warrants
themselves continue

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:56.620
this country's brutal and
often deadly regulation

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:58.540
of the black intimate sphere.

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:01.170
Now, as the organization, and I say,

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:03.940
as the organization Survived and Punished,

00:25:03.940 --> 00:25:08.380
co-founded by the
incomparable Mariame Kaba,

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:10.290
abolitionist, organizer,

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:13.600
this organization argues
effectively that jails and prisons

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:16.130
and not rape crisis centers,
and women's shelters

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:19.000
actually represent our
collective institutional response

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:21.200
to the problem of gender-based
violence against black

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:22.970
and other marginalized women.

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:25.440
So the SCLU estimates that almost 60%

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:27.880
of people women's prisons worldwide

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:29.220
are survivors of violence.

00:25:29.220 --> 00:25:33.570
And at some prisons, there's
numbers as high as 94%.

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:35.320
Now, I wanna be clear
to mark the distinction

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:37.350
between sex and sexual violence here.

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:39.250
Though I also wanna be clear that women

00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:40.900
are experiencing sexual violence

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:42.420
in this institutional context

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:46.070
because they are in intimate
relationships and have had sex.

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:48.980
I wanna mark the fact that
black women are being murdered,

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:51.250
killed, severely hurt, and punished

00:25:51.250 --> 00:25:53.307
ultimately because they have had sex,

00:25:53.307 --> 00:25:55.390
and this creates significant political

00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:57.720
and organizational problems.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:01.040
But my research looks
not only at this aspect,

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:05.480
but also at what I've come to
call black women's death work.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:10.264
So here, women like Ida B.
Wells, Mamie Till Bradley,

00:26:10.264 --> 00:26:13.200
that is Emmett Till's mom,
and Clementine Barfield

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:15.370
of Detroit's Save Our Sons and Daughters,

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:19.020
also known as SOSAD,
which is an organization

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:21.980
of the mothers of murdered
children founded in the 1980s

00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:23.990
and connected to our current era

00:26:23.990 --> 00:26:26.100
and its own politics of death.

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:28.650
As Trayvon Martin's
mother contacted SOSAD's

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:30.770
Philadelphia chapter,
they were among the first

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:34.040
to bring attention to
the death of her son.

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:37.150
So each of these women made
transformational contributions

00:26:37.150 --> 00:26:39.040
to the black counter public sphere

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:40.710
and their entry into that public sphere

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:43.590
was authorized by a man's death.

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:48.520
Now, the subtitle for my
section on Madam Wells

00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:51.550
is Wells and the Empirical Miracle.

00:26:51.550 --> 00:26:54.650
And I talk about how she
constructs a black truth apparatus

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:56.350
from broken white parts.

00:26:56.350 --> 00:26:58.600
And she's the first to
use a lynching photo

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:00.120
in anti-lynching work,

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:03.630
a strategy that we know will
be repeated for years to come,

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:06.920
but she also commissions
a portrait of herself

00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:10.030
with her murdered friend,
Thomas Moss's family

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:12.350
and Lee Rayford says that this represents

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:15.460
her important expansion of
the lynching photo genre,

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:17.190
bringing the family left behind

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:20.170
into the frame of anti-lynching activism.

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:24.820
Now, I'm in the process
writing the section

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:26.380
on Barfield and SOSAD,

00:27:26.380 --> 00:27:29.010
and I could literally go
on for hours about them,

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:32.310
but let me just say that the
organization was extraordinary.

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:34.597
So two weeks after one
of her sons was killed

00:27:34.597 --> 00:27:38.010
and another gravely injured
in an active teen violence,

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:40.420
Barfield organized a meeting
for families affected

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:42.890
by violence against children and teens.

00:27:42.890 --> 00:27:45.550
The resulting organization became,

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:47.380
according to youth violence expert

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:48.530
and former assistant dean

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:52.230
of Harvard School of Public
Health, Deborah Prothrow-Stith,

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.980
I quote, a model for
community based activism

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:56.860
for organizing for the
rest of the country.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:59.010
SOSAD would go on to have an impact

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:02.130
on national conversations
regarding youth violence

00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:04.310
and at its peak, receive requests

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:07.390
for organizing help from
Milwaukee, St. Louis, Atlanta,

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:10.860
Los Angeles, San Francisco,
Columbus, and Omaha,

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:12.700
chapters were started in Flint, Michigan,

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:15.453
Fresno, Birmingham,
Alabama, and St. Louis,

00:28:17.952 --> 00:28:19.720
and I told you about the
Philadelphia chapter,

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:22.630
which was the largest and
perhaps most important.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:26.080
Now they ran a grief support group

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:29.800
for the mothers of murdered children.

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:31.040
The support group would expand

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:32.650
to include a family support group,

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:35.440
a male grief support group,
a child counseling group,

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:37.333
a survivors of violence group.

00:28:38.260 --> 00:28:40.070
They ran a 24-hour crisis highline

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:43.350
and provided mental health
services for children who hurt.

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:45.610
They organized a crisis intervention team

00:28:45.610 --> 00:28:47.120
that went into schools, churches,

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:50.170
and door-to-door neighborhoods
after area death.

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:51.820
They had a speakers bureau,

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:53.550
a monthly newsletter with columns

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:56.830
by Barfield and James
Boggs, a weekly radio show.

00:28:56.830 --> 00:28:58.790
They kickstarted the
Urban Gardening Movement

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:00.100
with their harvesting program,

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:02.640
and they even organized in prisons.

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:05.820
Now, members of the organization
held an explicitly theory

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:08.360
of what allowed violence to
proliferate in the community,

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:11.430
and that was the seating
of public state space.

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:12.377
So Barfield would say,

00:29:12.377 --> 00:29:15.147
"Instead of hiding behind
barred doors and windows,

00:29:15.147 --> 00:29:18.617
"we can and must march out
of our homes and our churches

00:29:18.617 --> 00:29:22.027
"and begin walking the streets
in large and small groups,

00:29:22.027 --> 00:29:23.967
"making clear to everyone, young and old,

00:29:23.967 --> 00:29:25.707
"that we are reclaiming our communities,

00:29:25.707 --> 00:29:26.697
"looking out for each other

00:29:26.697 --> 00:29:29.240
"and saving our sons and daughters."

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:32.097
Earlier in the same
column, Barfield would say,

00:29:32.097 --> 00:29:34.097
"Every time one of our sons
and daughters is killed,

00:29:34.097 --> 00:29:36.497
"we must march, we must
hold candlelight vigils,

00:29:36.497 --> 00:29:39.527
"we must create people's
memorials to show that we care.

00:29:39.527 --> 00:29:42.727
"We must not allow Daniel Barr,
Gary Irwin and Otis Haley,

00:29:42.727 --> 00:29:44.237
"or any of our lost loved ones

00:29:44.237 --> 00:29:46.557
"to be turned into faceless statistics.

00:29:46.557 --> 00:29:48.527
"On street corners like Joy and Evergreen,

00:29:48.527 --> 00:29:50.810
"we must paint their
portraits to remind us

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:53.657
"that each one of them was someone's son,

00:29:53.657 --> 00:29:56.257
"someone's brother, someone's friend."

00:29:56.257 --> 00:29:58.390
"We have to physically
plan ourselves," she said,

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:01.357
and she would make
reference to trees a lot,

00:30:01.357 --> 00:30:03.337
"at the scene of every
shooting or homicide

00:30:03.337 --> 00:30:05.997
"in our neighborhood, we
must make hope visible.

00:30:05.997 --> 00:30:08.367
"Our children need to
see with their own eyes

00:30:08.367 --> 00:30:09.587
"in the midst of devastation

00:30:09.587 --> 00:30:10.927
"that people are standing together

00:30:10.927 --> 00:30:13.120
"and struggling for a better world,"

00:30:13.120 --> 00:30:14.810
but this was not all, right?

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:16.877
So she says, "We have to organize

00:30:16.877 --> 00:30:18.177
"to rebuild our communities.

00:30:18.177 --> 00:30:19.387
"Everyone can do something,

00:30:19.387 --> 00:30:21.487
"we could march against the
crack house on our block,

00:30:21.487 --> 00:30:24.217
"we can develop treatment programs,

00:30:24.217 --> 00:30:27.157
"we can transform vacant
lots into community gardens

00:30:27.157 --> 00:30:30.077
"and children's parks where
kids can play together.

00:30:30.077 --> 00:30:33.127
"We can work towards
creating small businesses

00:30:33.127 --> 00:30:34.577
"to provide jobs.

00:30:34.577 --> 00:30:36.557
"We can organize block
parties and festivals

00:30:36.557 --> 00:30:39.990
"to celebrate the positive
achievements of youth."

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:43.310
Now justice then was not about punishment,

00:30:43.310 --> 00:30:45.280
but the work of building a better world.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:47.910
Justice for the women's dead children

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:49.860
was bringing about an end to death worlds

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:52.340
and creating life worlds in their place.

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:56.100
Communing, however, with
death world, dead children,

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:58.840
even as they built these life worlds.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:00.500
So significantly, they had

00:31:00.500 --> 00:31:02.440
a much more sophisticated understanding

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:03.690
of the causes of violence,

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:07.870
and at times an implicit,
but often explicit critique

00:31:07.870 --> 00:31:10.270
of the war on drugs and
the carceral state approach

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:11.340
to violence.

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:12.790
Additionally, they embraced

00:31:12.790 --> 00:31:14.940
a form of community accountability

00:31:14.940 --> 00:31:18.580
that is endorsed by
contemporary abolitionists.

00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:22.207
So Melinda Price who's written
on the organization says,

00:31:22.207 --> 00:31:24.067
"Through their relationship
with their children,

00:31:24.067 --> 00:31:25.720
"both dead and living,"

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:28.790
and here, Price is referring
to what she's calling

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:30.780
the afterlife of black motherhood,

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:32.483
where these organizations,

00:31:33.590 --> 00:31:36.160
organized mothers of
murdered children continue

00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:39.210
to parent their children by
creating nurturing communities

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:41.740
for kids amidst the
violence and destruction

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:43.960
of urban abandonment in the war on drugs,

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:46.130
that is life worlds in
the midst of death worlds,

00:31:46.130 --> 00:31:47.537
but Price continues,

00:31:47.537 --> 00:31:50.847
"They crafted public roles
to solve complex problems

00:31:50.847 --> 00:31:51.680
"at its roots.

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:55.117
"In this sense, their analysis
was more complex and astute

00:31:55.117 --> 00:31:58.457
"than the analysis that
informed any government program.

00:31:58.457 --> 00:32:00.837
"They understood that solving
the problem of violence

00:32:00.837 --> 00:32:03.157
"against and among black
children meant surrounding them

00:32:03.157 --> 00:32:05.667
"with love, building healthy self-esteem,

00:32:05.667 --> 00:32:08.237
"and finding meaningful work for teenagers

00:32:08.237 --> 00:32:10.470
"on the cusp of adulthood."

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:13.660
So I'm thinking about the
relationship between Wells,

00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:17.310
Till Bradley, and Barfield's
death work for racial justice

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:19.890
and the death work that must
be done for the women who die

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:22.170
because they have had sex today.

00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:23.870
Now, someone pointed out the difference

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:25.930
in the public's acceptance
of grief in public

00:32:25.930 --> 00:32:28.680
for the mothers of a murdered child

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:32.370
and their life world
creating grief in public,

00:32:32.370 --> 00:32:35.830
and the difference between that, right,

00:32:35.830 --> 00:32:39.330
and grief in public for
women who've had sex.

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:41.460
And I think this is an important point.

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:42.790
But as I've thought about it,

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:45.000
I realize that Price reminds us

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:46.670
that at the time of SOSAD's work,

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:49.900
the predominant understandings
of the black mother

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:52.420
was the welfare queen and the crack queen.

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:55.150
So these women had no easy road to hope.

00:32:55.150 --> 00:32:57.780
And perhaps, the gap
between these two archetypes

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:01.010
is not as far apart as we might think.

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:02.430
In any case, just this week

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:04.770
I became struck by the
similarity of Barfield

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.500
and Kaba's theory of what justice demands.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:09.420
So across three to four decades,

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.840
they seem to have come
to the same conclusion.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:14.280
And you may or may not
be surprised to learn

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:17.260
that it aligns with the
conclusions of Iris Marion Young,

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.790
that justice requires participation,

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:21.240
participatory democracy,

00:33:21.240 --> 00:33:22.810
that we cannot create something good

00:33:22.810 --> 00:33:25.770
with which we are not
all involved in making.

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:29.000
So institutionally, the tough
on crime approach must end

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.710
for there to be sexual justice,

00:33:30.710 --> 00:33:34.510
but we must all be involved in
the creating of institutions.

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:38.520
I can't say what I think
sexual justice will look like.

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:40.390
Like Barfield and Kaba, I believe

00:33:40.390 --> 00:33:42.780
that we all have to say
that collectively together.

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:46.040
And I do believe that like
them, we can't have it

00:33:46.040 --> 00:33:48.690
if we abdicate our
collective responsibility

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:51.097
for providing sexual safety for us all.

00:33:51.097 --> 00:33:52.830
But I do think that it will involve

00:33:52.830 --> 00:33:55.400
all of us creating life worlds of the kind

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:58.100
that Barfield's organization
attempted to do.

00:33:58.100 --> 00:33:59.222
Thank you.

00:33:59.222 --> 00:34:02.139
(crowd applauding)

00:34:11.147 --> 00:34:12.000
- All right, I'm gonna stand up,

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:13.540
but I don't have any slides,

00:34:13.540 --> 00:34:15.073
but I thought I'd stand.

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:19.080
Okay, so I'm talking about sexual justice

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:24.080
and I'm taking sexual
down to its its root, sex,

00:34:24.430 --> 00:34:28.960
and by sex, I mean sex as an M or F or X,

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:30.750
not as sex as in having sex.

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.150
So that's my sex part.

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:36.440
The justice, I think, we'll
get to around the end.

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:40.230
But one other short note on language,

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:42.280
many people would use gender

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:45.780
to talk about the marks
on our driver's license

00:34:45.780 --> 00:34:47.690
and the passports and so on.

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:50.333
But I use the word sex,
and let me explain why.

00:34:51.350 --> 00:34:52.840
This is the thing, I can either see you

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:54.613
or I can see my paper, so
I think I'll see my paper

00:34:54.613 --> 00:34:57.503
and you all blurry, oh, well.
- Or look at folks here.

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:03.120
- Okay, so a lot of my work is focused

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:06.440
on how state actors define the word sex

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:10.473
or decide who's M or who's F or who's X.

00:35:11.663 --> 00:35:15.830
And I am not interested in
evaluating the correctness

00:35:15.830 --> 00:35:19.250
of different claims to
deciding on who's M or F or X.

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:20.730
So what I do is I look at

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:22.390
what different definitions make possible.

00:35:22.390 --> 00:35:23.223
So I don't know what sex is.

00:35:23.223 --> 00:35:24.570
I don't know what the right thing is.

00:35:24.570 --> 00:35:26.490
I'm just like, let's see what,

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:29.610
a definition that a government
agency puts forward,

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:33.106
that's back by the force of
law, what effects that has.

00:35:33.106 --> 00:35:37.410
So I use gender though, I
still use the word gender,

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:42.410
but I refer to, by gender
I refer to like shared,

00:35:42.790 --> 00:35:45.340
though often contested
norms, narratives, practices,

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:49.650
and conventions that are age,
bodies, identities, roles,

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:51.670
and expressions in
hierarchies of different

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:53.690
based on binary notions
of male and female,

00:35:53.690 --> 00:35:56.420
masculine and feminine and so on.

00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:58.630
So just a little introduction.

00:35:58.630 --> 00:36:01.860
So in the last month,
Supreme Court nominee,

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:06.840
Ketanji Brown Jackson, was
castigate by a Republican Senator

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:09.180
for refusing to define the word woman

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:10.490
in her nomination hearings.

00:36:10.490 --> 00:36:14.130
A week later, the Biden
administration announced

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:17.100
that individuals will have
the option of choosing X

00:36:17.100 --> 00:36:19.270
as the gender marker on their passport.

00:36:19.270 --> 00:36:20.820
I should say sex marker, sorry?

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:24.073
So it's quite a weird moment or act.

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:27.420
Though the right and the left
are like bitterly divided,

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:30.770
by the left, I mean the
good left, my kind of left,

00:36:30.770 --> 00:36:34.200
not the old lefty boys who
don't like trans people,

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:37.410
whether the right and the
left are brutally divided

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:39.400
over the rights of transgender people,

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:42.080
activists on both sides
agree on one thing,

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:45.010
if their side can win
the battle of how society

00:36:45.010 --> 00:36:47.330
as a whole defines a person's sex,

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:49.970
Americans will be forced
to either give up,

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:51.470
discriminate against trans people,

00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:54.420
or be allowed to continue
discriminate against trans people.

00:36:54.420 --> 00:36:57.470
So it's like, the way the
rhetoric kind of moves around,

00:36:57.470 --> 00:37:00.750
it's as the faded republic
depends in no small measure

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:03.200
on the outcome of the
battle over sex definition.

00:37:04.620 --> 00:37:08.700
But this notion of a
kind of mannequin battle

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:10.500
between the sexes, however,

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:15.500
rests on a dramatically
mistaken view of how state,

00:37:15.590 --> 00:37:18.230
federal, and local
governments approach the issue

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:20.020
of deciding a person's sex.

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:21.400
Whether it's on a birth certificate,

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:23.630
a driver's license, a
marriage certificate,

00:37:23.630 --> 00:37:24.850
whether it's in a prison,

00:37:24.850 --> 00:37:27.070
a homeless shelter or school bathroom,

00:37:27.070 --> 00:37:28.830
historically, government agencies

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:32.170
have been far less interested
in social conventions

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:33.510
about what sex is,

00:37:33.510 --> 00:37:36.750
whether it's biological
assignment at birth,

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:39.428
gender identity as an adult and so on.

00:37:39.428 --> 00:37:42.220
They're less interested
in the perfect definition

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:44.320
in some abstract platonic way

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:48.400
than what sex does for
that agency's mission.

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:50.060
So officials in prison, for example,

00:37:50.060 --> 00:37:51.810
define sex differently than do officials

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:53.620
in the department of motor vehicles.

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:56.520
Even when those officials
work in the same jurisdiction

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:58.500
of a single city or a state.

00:37:58.500 --> 00:38:00.780
So sex classifications radically depend

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:02.400
in ways that few journalists

00:38:04.156 --> 00:38:07.100
and scholars and politicians recognize,

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:09.563
except we do 'cause we're in this world,

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:14.010
sex classifications depend
on what a government agency

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:16.080
is trying to do.

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:17.370
So I wanna kind of illustrate this

00:38:17.370 --> 00:38:19.170
by telling a story of New York City.

00:38:19.170 --> 00:38:21.510
And I'm the kid at the
Thanksgiving dinner table

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:23.740
who like started to tell a story,

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:25.780
and by the time I got to the
end, no was paying attention.

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:29.170
So in my '50s, I'm still like,
maybe I can tell a story,

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:31.030
but here I have a microphone,

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:33.010
and you probably won't interrupt me,

00:38:33.010 --> 00:38:35.030
but I'm gonna try to tell a story

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:37.990
and not mess up any punchlines.

00:38:37.990 --> 00:38:41.820
So in the 1960s, trans
people were going to the city

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:44.410
and saying, "Hey, will you
change our birth certificates?"

00:38:44.410 --> 00:38:46.200
And the city was like, "Wow,
I don't know, that's weird."

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:47.770
So they did it a few
times, and after a while,

00:38:47.770 --> 00:38:50.270
they were like, "I'm not
sure this is a good idea."

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:51.990
So they asked a bunch of doctors,

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:53.610
should we change their birth certificates?

00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:57.977
The doctors met or three
times, and they were like, "No.

00:38:57.977 --> 00:39:00.027
"Because if you let them change
their birth certificates,

00:39:00.027 --> 00:39:02.283
"they might commit a
fraud upon the public,

00:39:03.917 --> 00:39:06.641
"by like marrying some
unsuspecting person,"

00:39:06.641 --> 00:39:09.620
their image was of a trans woman marrying

00:39:09.620 --> 00:39:11.400
an unsuspecting cisgender man

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:14.260
who would accidentally get
married to a trans woman.

00:39:14.260 --> 00:39:15.510
So they wouldn't change it.

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:17.790
Then in the 1970s, I think it was the SOU

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:20.480
or the New York Civil Liberties Union,

00:39:20.480 --> 00:39:22.650
tried to get the city
to change its policy.

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:23.680
And the city did.

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:25.307
It said, "Okay, this is a weird issue.

00:39:25.307 --> 00:39:26.140
"You know what we're gonna do?

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:28.327
"We're gonna give you
new birth certificates,

00:39:28.327 --> 00:39:32.090
"but we're gonna take the
box that indicates sex off."

00:39:32.090 --> 00:39:33.550
There's no box whatsoever.

00:39:33.550 --> 00:39:35.017
So everybody else has
a box that says M or F,

00:39:35.017 --> 00:39:36.690
and trans people's birth certificates,

00:39:36.690 --> 00:39:38.700
people who were born in
the city, there's no box,

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:40.480
which isn't great for people

00:39:40.480 --> 00:39:41.730
who had to present their
birth certificates,

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:43.660
'cause people who look at
birth certificates all day

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:45.330
would say, well, this is a trans person,

00:39:45.330 --> 00:39:48.640
'cause there's no box here,
but anyways, it was something.

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:50.660
But by the 2000s, New York City,

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:52.550
thinking of itself as
very progressive thought,

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:53.900
okay, we're a little behind.

00:39:53.900 --> 00:39:55.790
In the 70s, this might
have been a cool policy,

00:39:55.790 --> 00:39:56.980
but now of this is behind.

00:39:56.980 --> 00:39:58.860
So they convened a committee,

00:39:58.860 --> 00:40:02.260
and this is where I come
into it as like an expert,

00:40:02.260 --> 00:40:04.410
but probably more of
an advocate, actually.

00:40:05.350 --> 00:40:07.150
They convened a committee to decide like,

00:40:07.150 --> 00:40:09.257
okay, what should the right policy be

00:40:09.257 --> 00:40:13.400
for letting people change their sex marker

00:40:13.400 --> 00:40:14.840
in their birth certificate.

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:16.610
So they had trans healthcare advocates,

00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:18.210
transgender rights advocates.

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:20.793
They had surgeons, they had psychiatrists,

00:40:22.550 --> 00:40:24.980
and of the people who were
not on the city's payroll,

00:40:24.980 --> 00:40:26.500
so some of the people were city officials,

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:29.070
but the people who weren't
on the city's payroll,

00:40:29.070 --> 00:40:31.860
everybody worked hard to
convince policy makers

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:34.760
to drop the requirement
for any kind of surgical

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:36.300
or medical intervention.

00:40:36.300 --> 00:40:38.370
The only people who
didn't work for the city

00:40:38.370 --> 00:40:43.250
who wanted to keep a surgical
requirement were surgeons.

00:40:43.250 --> 00:40:44.610
They thought that was a good metric

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:46.290
for changing people's sex.

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:47.500
The good thing is they made

00:40:47.500 --> 00:40:48.830
so much money in private practice,

00:40:48.830 --> 00:40:51.130
coming to a meeting cost them
a lot of money was presumed,

00:40:51.130 --> 00:40:52.340
so they did like came to one meeting,

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:54.543
so that was actually quite helpful.

00:40:55.990 --> 00:40:57.340
But we kind of made the usual argument,

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:59.470
transition is an individualized process

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:01.410
and gender identity, not genitals,

00:41:01.410 --> 00:41:03.720
should determine sex classification.

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:05.360
And initially, the Board of Health,

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:06.590
which is the group we were working with,

00:41:06.590 --> 00:41:09.770
agreed to adopt this recommendation.

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:13.460
But then they shopped it
around to other city agencies,

00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:15.337
and the other city agencies said,

00:41:15.337 --> 00:41:18.240
"No, this is a terrible
policy, we don't agree."

00:41:18.240 --> 00:41:22.720
And our proposal was
shelved for some years.

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:25.610
So we decided, the advocates
on the committee decided

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:27.500
that this is just plain transphobic.

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:28.780
This is like not okay.

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:29.873
This is just mean.

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:33.610
We would talk about the
harms that people face

00:41:33.610 --> 00:41:36.200
when there is a mismatch
between the M or the F

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:39.233
on a person's identity papers
and their gender identity.

00:41:40.730 --> 00:41:43.000
There's a great quote,
one woman who testified

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:44.467
to the city officials said, she says,

00:41:44.467 --> 00:41:46.427
"I don't suffer from gender dysphoria.

00:41:46.427 --> 00:41:48.600
"I suffer from bureaucratic dysphoria."

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:52.150
She kind of talks about the
problem is the state's problem.

00:41:52.150 --> 00:41:53.470
So that's like very powerful,

00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:56.360
and I was there when
that statement was made.

00:41:56.360 --> 00:41:59.310
But one thing that my
thinking has shifted on this

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:02.640
is that focusing on the
harm to transgender people

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:05.710
that these decisions make,
actually in some sense,

00:42:05.710 --> 00:42:08.090
makes it hard to see
what the city was doing

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:11.420
and what their reasoning was.

00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:12.950
It just seemed transphobic.

00:42:12.950 --> 00:42:14.820
But as a few years later,
I was doing some research,

00:42:14.820 --> 00:42:16.070
I came across some material

00:42:16.070 --> 00:42:17.690
that provided a different perspective.

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:19.380
So back in the '60s when the New York City

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:20.860
was trying to figure out what to do,

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:22.580
they wrote some official in Washington.

00:42:22.580 --> 00:42:23.720
They said, well, what do you do,

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:26.920
you people at the Bureau of Statistics

00:42:26.920 --> 00:42:27.980
or somebody at the Department of Health,

00:42:27.980 --> 00:42:30.040
Education, and Welfare,

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:33.297
and the federal government
people wrote back and said,

00:42:33.297 --> 00:42:36.617
"We thought about this and
I canvased a bunch of people

00:42:36.617 --> 00:42:38.347
"from other departments
and we can provide you

00:42:38.347 --> 00:42:40.290
"with no guidance on the question."

00:42:40.290 --> 00:42:42.567
He said, "Since various agencies carry out

00:42:42.567 --> 00:42:44.177
"differing responsibilities,

00:42:44.177 --> 00:42:46.567
"the problems which confront them vary.

00:42:46.567 --> 00:42:47.967
"The more we delved into the problem,

00:42:47.967 --> 00:42:50.040
"the more ramifications that corrupt up."

00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:52.780
So this for me was like looking
at sex classification stuff,

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:54.740
it's so fascinating, 'cause
they're based talking about

00:42:54.740 --> 00:42:56.680
how sex is an instrument
of governmentality,

00:42:56.680 --> 00:42:58.900
though that's another
language they were using,

00:42:58.900 --> 00:43:02.003
and not talking about sex is
in some kind of abstract thing.

00:43:03.490 --> 00:43:05.150
So officials at some agencies

00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:06.790
like the Social Security Administration

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:08.140
were worried about identity management

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:09.670
and social security benefits.

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:11.170
They were worried, the
Selective Service people

00:43:11.170 --> 00:43:12.160
were worried, all the
different agencies were like,

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:14.380
"Oh, I'm not sure about this."

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:16.750
So in other words, there
could be no unified policy

00:43:16.750 --> 00:43:18.190
on the matter of sex reclassification

00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:20.430
because each agency
would have to investigate

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:23.100
the effects of sex
reclassification policies

00:43:23.100 --> 00:43:24.370
on its own work.

00:43:24.370 --> 00:43:28.723
And then back to the
2005 policy that failed,

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:31.700
a transgender rights group sued the city.

00:43:31.700 --> 00:43:34.070
They said, "This is not
okay, this is mean."

00:43:34.070 --> 00:43:37.610
They said that the requiring
individuals to have surgery

00:43:37.610 --> 00:43:40.810
before their birth
certificate could be changed

00:43:40.810 --> 00:43:43.660
was arbitrary, capricious, discriminatory,

00:43:43.660 --> 00:43:46.430
and otherwise unlawful, and
the law, which is boring,

00:43:46.430 --> 00:43:47.480
but in the law, it's like it's bad

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:48.750
to be arbitrary and capricious,

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:50.843
they're supposed to be a
good reason for things.

00:43:52.490 --> 00:43:54.600
So the advocates were drawing
on medical authorities

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:57.170
to argue that sex markers
and identity documents

00:43:57.170 --> 00:44:00.120
should be consistent with the
individual's gender identity,

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:03.020
drawing on all this like social
science and medical science.

00:44:03.020 --> 00:44:07.390
So the city's policy according
to advocates was irrational.

00:44:07.390 --> 00:44:08.960
And the response of the city's lawyers

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:11.120
kind of goes back to the
response of that federal official

00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:12.590
in the 1960s.

00:44:12.590 --> 00:44:15.070
They argued that the existence
of different approaches

00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:16.580
to sex classification

00:44:16.580 --> 00:44:19.560
does not render an
agency's rule irrational.

00:44:19.560 --> 00:44:22.020
For the city, the rationality
of each agency's approach

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:25.190
to sex classification depended
on what the agency did

00:44:25.190 --> 00:44:29.270
on its remit, not on
what sex is in itself.

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:31.840
So the debate on sex was taking place

00:44:31.840 --> 00:44:32.900
in two different registers.

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:34.880
So the advocates including myself,

00:44:34.880 --> 00:44:36.810
we were thinking about experts and truth,

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:39.287
and how to really think about what sex is.

00:44:39.287 --> 00:44:41.730
And the bureaucrats were
thinking about governing,

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:44.220
about like what a sex
classification would do to a prison

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:46.320
or homeless shelter or a different agency.

00:44:47.830 --> 00:44:49.910
So for the city,

00:44:49.910 --> 00:44:53.783
how sex was defined could
be changed by agency

00:44:55.310 --> 00:44:56.390
depending on the need.

00:44:56.390 --> 00:44:59.100
So I like to tell this story
because it shows how advocates,

00:44:59.100 --> 00:45:02.220
including myself, working on this issue

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:04.290
went into the process,
thinking the point was degree

00:45:04.290 --> 00:45:06.850
on some right definition of sex,

00:45:06.850 --> 00:45:08.480
but it turned out that
the city bureaucrats

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:10.060
and lawyers were the real facadeans,

00:45:10.060 --> 00:45:12.270
understanding that sex
was not a thing in itself,

00:45:12.270 --> 00:45:13.860
but something instrumentalized differently

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:15.750
by different agencies.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:18.940
So, so much scholarship and
advocacy has been taken up

00:45:18.940 --> 00:45:20.880
with arguing that it's unjust

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:22.580
to use traditional ideas about gender

00:45:22.580 --> 00:45:24.890
to require transgender
people to have surgery

00:45:24.890 --> 00:45:27.050
before changing their sex classification

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:28.170
or to prevent any changes at all.

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:29.990
A lot of like, that is just wrong,

00:45:29.990 --> 00:45:32.330
and I agree with that, it is wrong.

00:45:32.330 --> 00:45:34.680
But from these precincts, we
also hear that sex and gender

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:36.840
are so complicated that
the only workable solution

00:45:36.840 --> 00:45:39.590
is to mix base sex classification
on gender identity.

00:45:39.590 --> 00:45:41.970
I don't disagree with that either,

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:44.770
but the other assumption that bad policies

00:45:44.770 --> 00:45:46.490
are the result of transphobia

00:45:46.490 --> 00:45:49.730
and that good policies are
not doesn't help us understand

00:45:49.730 --> 00:45:53.140
how every one of them fixes us in place,

00:45:53.140 --> 00:45:55.010
implicates us in a relation to state power

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:57.950
that might allow some reforms
of sex classification,

00:45:57.950 --> 00:46:00.530
but draws our attention
away from the other half

00:46:00.530 --> 00:46:02.640
of the equation, state power.

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:05.100
So we spend a lot of
time denaturalizing sex,

00:46:05.100 --> 00:46:08.190
but like we're not so good
at denaturalizing the state.

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:09.480
You know what I mean?

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:11.290
No one reads marks anymore.

00:46:11.290 --> 00:46:14.290
I mean, maybe maybe it's
getting turned in reasonably,

00:46:14.290 --> 00:46:15.970
but so the proposition,

00:46:15.970 --> 00:46:17.620
and I was doing this paper this morning,

00:46:17.620 --> 00:46:19.080
I was like, boy, like every paper

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:20.470
a political theorist ever presents.

00:46:20.470 --> 00:46:21.590
it's really just another version

00:46:21.590 --> 00:46:23.480
of Marx's "On the Jewish Question".

00:46:23.480 --> 00:46:25.130
So I apologize for that,

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:26.440
I guess that's the tradition I'm in,

00:46:26.440 --> 00:46:29.630
but the proposition
that sex is manufactured

00:46:29.630 --> 00:46:33.490
in and through regulations,
formal and informal policies,

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:36.950
judicial decisions, and legislative
enactments does not mean

00:46:36.950 --> 00:46:39.540
that we should see the
state as fixed in place,

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:41.250
still and hard as a monument.

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:43.613
That sex is an output doesn't mean

00:46:43.613 --> 00:46:46.130
that we should black box the state,

00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:47.920
like turn into a thing that we don't look

00:46:47.920 --> 00:46:49.473
into its inner doings.

00:46:51.637 --> 00:46:54.920
So before I conclude, one last word

00:46:54.920 --> 00:46:56.050
about the New York City situation,

00:46:56.050 --> 00:46:57.650
New York City now has a great policy.

00:46:57.650 --> 00:47:00.430
You can change it to F to M to X,

00:47:00.430 --> 00:47:01.450
you don't need a doctor's letter,

00:47:01.450 --> 00:47:03.340
you don't need any medical intervention.

00:47:03.340 --> 00:47:06.560
That's all really, it's
the perfect policy.

00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:09.070
But it's not like the city finally agreed

00:47:09.070 --> 00:47:11.140
that sex really should be gender identity.

00:47:11.140 --> 00:47:13.540
It was because the new
logic of identity politics

00:47:13.540 --> 00:47:16.510
meant that the de Blasio administration

00:47:16.510 --> 00:47:19.930
could give this
constituency what it wanted.

00:47:19.930 --> 00:47:21.387
He said, "I can give you,"
in the press release,

00:47:21.387 --> 00:47:23.540
"We're getting transgender
people what they want."

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:26.930
But everybody else born the
city still gets an M or an F

00:47:26.930 --> 00:47:28.290
on their birth certificate.

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:29.640
So we're still kind of trapped

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:31.400
in this arithmetic of identity politics

00:47:31.400 --> 00:47:33.780
of demands made and demands met.

00:47:33.780 --> 00:47:36.650
So to conclude, to return
to the powerless moment

00:47:36.650 --> 00:47:38.380
in which we find ourselves,

00:47:38.380 --> 00:47:40.990
where we have passage of
legislation targeting trans youth

00:47:40.990 --> 00:47:42.320
and those who care for them,

00:47:42.320 --> 00:47:44.720
bills excluding trans girls from sports,

00:47:44.720 --> 00:47:46.930
don't say gay bills, the organized attack

00:47:46.930 --> 00:47:49.220
on what's taken to be gender ideology,

00:47:49.220 --> 00:47:50.710
I mean that's more
outside the United States,

00:47:50.710 --> 00:47:53.520
but as scary as this moment is,

00:47:53.520 --> 00:47:56.560
I see two positive or exciting,

00:47:56.560 --> 00:47:58.610
hopefully, they're exciting possibilities.

00:47:58.610 --> 00:48:02.700
First, sex classification has
always been instrumentalized

00:48:02.700 --> 00:48:05.830
for the purposes of governance,
we're already there.

00:48:05.830 --> 00:48:08.350
But what was previously
a relatively secretive

00:48:08.350 --> 00:48:11.260
internal decision of government
bureaucrats concerned

00:48:11.260 --> 00:48:13.390
with the efficiency and
effectiveness of their agencies

00:48:13.390 --> 00:48:15.260
in particular state projects

00:48:15.260 --> 00:48:18.660
has very recently become a
boisterous public debate.

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:20.580
For the first time, the
decision about gender identity

00:48:20.580 --> 00:48:22.510
has the potential to be democratic,

00:48:22.510 --> 00:48:24.270
not only deliberated by the people,

00:48:24.270 --> 00:48:25.610
but with the interests or concerns

00:48:25.610 --> 00:48:28.000
of the people most
affected by those policies,

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:30.440
trans people taken into account.

00:48:30.440 --> 00:48:34.000
Second, as we put the needs
of transgender people front

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:37.470
and center in deliberations
about sex classification,

00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:39.570
it's also an opportunity
to shine the spotlight

00:48:39.570 --> 00:48:41.160
on the other side

00:48:41.160 --> 00:48:43.400
of this mutually
constitutive relationship,

00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:44.960
the governing opposite apparatuses

00:48:44.960 --> 00:48:47.620
that determine what a
livable life looks like.

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:50.640
So we can get sex classification right.

00:48:50.640 --> 00:48:53.500
We can fight in the democratic
public sphere for that.

00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:54.390
That's good.

00:48:54.390 --> 00:48:56.060
But it's also important
to kind of point out

00:48:56.060 --> 00:48:59.480
like we can get everybody's,
every jurisdiction's,

00:48:59.480 --> 00:49:02.650
every agency's sex
classification policy right,

00:49:02.650 --> 00:49:06.170
but the changes that'd improve the lives

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:09.970
of the most trans people are
not those changes, right?

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:14.950
Those changes would be
having public health,

00:49:14.950 --> 00:49:18.680
a national healthcare
plan, prison abolition,

00:49:18.680 --> 00:49:20.510
and like radically reducing,

00:49:20.510 --> 00:49:22.150
if not eliminating income inequality.

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:25.123
Those are the things that would
change most people's lives.

00:49:27.530 --> 00:49:29.410
So as we contest the classifications,

00:49:29.410 --> 00:49:32.130
the exclusions from
public life, the attacks,

00:49:32.130 --> 00:49:34.170
the CPS investigations,

00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:37.340
this is also a moment to turn
the spotlight, not on sex,

00:49:37.340 --> 00:49:39.410
but back to the state and think about

00:49:39.410 --> 00:49:41.450
the very architecture of justice

00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:43.140
that distributes inequality.

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:44.462
So thank you.

00:49:44.462 --> 00:49:47.629
(audience applauding)

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:08.900
- So I wanna start by thanking
Joe and Gender Studies

00:50:08.900 --> 00:50:10.980
for inviting me to sit on this panel

00:50:10.980 --> 00:50:13.430
alongside three people whose work

00:50:13.430 --> 00:50:18.430
is tremendously influential,
both in my prior book

00:50:19.070 --> 00:50:22.923
and particularly in some
of what I'm working on now.

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:26.630
So I'm the one who's gonna talk about sex,

00:50:26.630 --> 00:50:28.940
because this is an LGBT studies thing,

00:50:28.940 --> 00:50:33.360
and it's time, it's time.

00:50:33.360 --> 00:50:34.820
We're gonna talk about,

00:50:34.820 --> 00:50:37.560
I'm gonna put the sex in sexual justice.

00:50:37.560 --> 00:50:39.310
So I'm a micro-sociologist,

00:50:39.310 --> 00:50:43.170
which means that I study
themes of intimacy recognition

00:50:44.820 --> 00:50:47.790
from a kind of perspective

00:50:47.790 --> 00:50:50.060
that foregrounds face to face interaction

00:50:50.060 --> 00:50:51.740
and thinking through the ways

00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:54.540
in which we live social structures

00:50:54.540 --> 00:50:57.240
in our most intimate lives and moments.

00:50:57.240 --> 00:51:00.300
And so I'm gonna be
talking a little bit today

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:04.280
about what sexual justice
demands of us interpersonally.

00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:05.670
So for the last several years

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:08.400
up until COVID made it impossible,

00:51:08.400 --> 00:51:10.360
I was conducting ethnographic research

00:51:10.360 --> 00:51:12.630
in several different BDSM communities.

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:15.510
Now I'm guessing that that if you're

00:51:15.510 --> 00:51:18.570
at an LGBT studies talk,

00:51:18.570 --> 00:51:22.300
this is not the first time
you've heard the term BDSM,

00:51:22.300 --> 00:51:25.620
but when I talk about it,
really what I'm focusing on

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:30.140
is sets of erotic practices
and emotional practices

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:35.680
that rely on a hierarchical
organization of power

00:51:35.680 --> 00:51:37.600
between people.

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:42.000
And as I began to do the writing

00:51:42.000 --> 00:51:44.980
of what will be this ethnographic book,

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:47.540
I realized that a lot
of what I was learning

00:51:47.540 --> 00:51:50.220
was about the erotic life
of social difference.

00:51:50.220 --> 00:51:52.960
How differences between
people related to gender,

00:51:52.960 --> 00:51:56.270
sexuality, race, class,
nationality, religion,

00:51:56.270 --> 00:52:00.840
and age become activated, even
electrified by the erotic,

00:52:00.840 --> 00:52:04.280
brought into focus,
crystallized in the moment.

00:52:04.280 --> 00:52:08.510
Now, the ways sexuality makes
gender turn technicolor,

00:52:08.510 --> 00:52:12.720
or makes racial difference animated,

00:52:12.720 --> 00:52:14.820
how social roles anchored in power

00:52:14.820 --> 00:52:16.550
like teacher-student, for example,

00:52:16.550 --> 00:52:18.950
which we were talking
about at lunch today,

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:20.460
gather intense affect.

00:52:20.460 --> 00:52:22.510
And usually, we think of these things

00:52:22.510 --> 00:52:23.810
in negative terms, right?

00:52:23.810 --> 00:52:27.280
As structures of power underlying
bodily forms of violence

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:31.213
like rape and psychic forms of
violence like sexual racism.

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:35.830
And these are deeply
important social pathologies.

00:52:35.830 --> 00:52:40.830
But on some level, I think
it lets nonviolent sex

00:52:41.080 --> 00:52:45.660
off the hook by way of
locating unjust sexuality

00:52:45.660 --> 00:52:48.620
only in moments of
non-consensual violence, right?

00:52:48.620 --> 00:52:51.210
Whether it's structural or interpersonal.

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:56.210
And so I wanna focus on two
epistemological responses

00:52:57.270 --> 00:53:01.380
to sort of transgressive sexuality

00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:02.930
that I think are complicit

00:53:02.930 --> 00:53:07.030
in this exceptionalizing of violence

00:53:07.030 --> 00:53:09.880
in non-consensual forms of sexuality.

00:53:09.880 --> 00:53:12.740
So the first is a framework
common in queer social

00:53:12.740 --> 00:53:14.370
and psychoanalytic theory

00:53:14.370 --> 00:53:17.980
that positions redemptive
sexuality as antisocial,

00:53:17.980 --> 00:53:20.440
anti-rational and ego shattering

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:23.000
to use Leo Bersani's formulation,

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:26.360
or the more popular often
attributed to feminist notion

00:53:26.360 --> 00:53:30.330
that sex is either presumptively
egalitarian and thus good,

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:32.480
or it sits in relation to trauma,

00:53:32.480 --> 00:53:35.700
as either it's self traumatic,
the cause of trauma,

00:53:35.700 --> 00:53:38.040
or a means of repairing it.

00:53:38.040 --> 00:53:40.580
And I wanna offer two
observations from my work,

00:53:40.580 --> 00:53:41.760
both of which are preliminary.

00:53:41.760 --> 00:53:44.040
See, here's the thing
about doing empirical work.

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:46.200
If you really, like real people keep you

00:53:46.200 --> 00:53:49.020
kind of intellectually
honest in a certain way.

00:53:49.020 --> 00:53:51.470
And so I think that they're often

00:53:51.470 --> 00:53:52.740
a good place to start, right?

00:53:52.740 --> 00:53:54.650
Like when we're thinking about
how things work in the world.

00:53:54.650 --> 00:53:57.160
So I wanna say two things today.

00:53:57.160 --> 00:53:59.360
First, I wanna argue for something

00:53:59.360 --> 00:54:02.090
that I'm coming to call
articulate intimacy,

00:54:02.090 --> 00:54:05.600
which one might even consider
a hyper rational orientation

00:54:05.600 --> 00:54:07.360
towards desire and pleasure.

00:54:07.360 --> 00:54:10.930
And second, I'm gonna suggest
that we think differently

00:54:10.930 --> 00:54:14.210
than queer theorists often
do about the connection

00:54:14.210 --> 00:54:17.223
between pleasure and
negative affect, right?

00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:22.930
That we think about ways to
imagine states beyond pleasure

00:54:22.930 --> 00:54:25.660
that facilitate eroticized
forms of intimacy

00:54:25.660 --> 00:54:28.770
that are deeply grounded
in social location.

00:54:28.770 --> 00:54:30.720
So BDSM is what sociologists call

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:33.740
an extreme case of intimate relation,

00:54:33.740 --> 00:54:36.250
in the sense that we view it
as deviant or exceptional,

00:54:36.250 --> 00:54:38.290
in the sense that we have
a sense that this is not

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:39.880
what most people do.

00:54:39.880 --> 00:54:42.360
But BDSM is also, as Ariana Cruz writes,

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:45.320
an aperture through which to
view some of the architecture

00:54:45.320 --> 00:54:47.510
of the sexual order more generally.

00:54:47.510 --> 00:54:50.020
So in my travels through BDSM subcultures,

00:54:50.020 --> 00:54:53.170
I met people who engaged in
various forms of psychological

00:54:53.170 --> 00:54:56.180
and erotic play that traffic
in social difference,

00:54:56.180 --> 00:55:00.090
race play, rape play, Nazi
play, these are some examples.

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:02.850
And in my conversations
with these practitioners,

00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:04.130
women and people of color

00:55:04.130 --> 00:55:07.640
had extremely articulate
relationships to their play,

00:55:07.640 --> 00:55:10.840
particularly those who occupy
the minoritarian positions

00:55:10.840 --> 00:55:13.310
in erotic encounters with others.

00:55:13.310 --> 00:55:16.900
Men and white people of
all genders struggled more

00:55:16.900 --> 00:55:19.370
with articulating their investments

00:55:19.370 --> 00:55:21.640
or individual level experiences

00:55:21.640 --> 00:55:24.860
with reproducing most
particularly racialized domination

00:55:24.860 --> 00:55:26.030
in their play.

00:55:26.030 --> 00:55:29.250
And I began thinking
about the inarticulateness

00:55:29.250 --> 00:55:32.810
of whiteness as perhaps
one of the biggest barriers

00:55:32.810 --> 00:55:35.430
to sexual justice on the dyadic level.

00:55:35.430 --> 00:55:39.620
And this is different from
what we hear about on NPR

00:55:39.620 --> 00:55:41.680
when they're talking about
white fragility, right?

00:55:41.680 --> 00:55:44.610
Which is a state of kind of
collapse into defensiveness

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:48.110
or anger at the challenge of
racial accountability, right?

00:55:48.110 --> 00:55:51.560
This is a failure of cultural
language for attempting,

00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:53.200
not merely accountability,

00:55:53.200 --> 00:55:56.390
but an accounting for
the racialized nature

00:55:56.390 --> 00:55:57.923
of all of our desires.

00:56:00.717 --> 00:56:04.450
And this wasn't only the
case for my subjects.

00:56:04.450 --> 00:56:06.540
This is true of dominant
queer theory as well.

00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:09.050
I'm thinking of the work of
Tim Dean, David Halperin,

00:56:09.050 --> 00:56:11.050
Leo Bersani and others,

00:56:11.050 --> 00:56:14.350
mostly coming from white
gay male perspectives,

00:56:14.350 --> 00:56:17.290
and some newer work in
psychoanalysis that foregrounds

00:56:17.290 --> 00:56:20.563
the dissolution of the
self in the act of sex,

00:56:22.450 --> 00:56:25.270
as kind of like the redemptive potential

00:56:25.270 --> 00:56:28.530
of transgressive sexuality.

00:56:28.530 --> 00:56:31.530
It's a kind of, and there's
a whole train of thought

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:36.480
in sociology as well that has
a kind of fantastical notion

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:39.700
of the sexual and sexual communities

00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:43.480
as spaces that are acutely amenable

00:56:43.480 --> 00:56:46.040
to transcending social difference, right?

00:56:46.040 --> 00:56:49.320
On the other hand, black
feminist writing by Ariana Cruz,

00:56:49.320 --> 00:56:53.030
Amber Musser, Jennifer
Nash, and others describes

00:56:53.030 --> 00:56:55.110
a form of black and brown Jewish songs

00:56:55.110 --> 00:56:57.640
that comes from a highly
racialized affect,

00:56:57.640 --> 00:56:59.810
from attending to the
pleasures that are possible

00:56:59.810 --> 00:57:02.460
by taking seriously the legacies of trauma

00:57:02.460 --> 00:57:05.560
and rapture that define
the current racial moment.

00:57:05.560 --> 00:57:09.040
Even by using negative
affect, not to aface the self,

00:57:09.040 --> 00:57:11.450
but to confront it more
deeply and honestly,

00:57:11.450 --> 00:57:13.900
than the sexual terrain
of egalitarianism allows.

00:57:15.740 --> 00:57:17.800
And I say this not as a judgment

00:57:17.800 --> 00:57:20.240
of my white subjects as individuals,

00:57:20.240 --> 00:57:22.680
many of whom were engaged
in earnest efforts

00:57:22.680 --> 00:57:24.970
toward accountability
in their intimate lives,

00:57:24.970 --> 00:57:27.680
and indeed BDSM in its ideal form,

00:57:27.680 --> 00:57:29.940
at least in the communities that I studied

00:57:29.940 --> 00:57:34.010
is itself a form of articulate
intimacy by which I mean,

00:57:34.010 --> 00:57:36.260
it is a form of relationship to others

00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:38.780
that has a meta narrative about itself.

00:57:38.780 --> 00:57:41.240
That accounts for specific
articulations of power

00:57:41.240 --> 00:57:43.890
that exist within a
structure of accountability

00:57:43.890 --> 00:57:46.120
that ensures not merely consent,

00:57:46.120 --> 00:57:49.070
but specific forms of self-reflection.

00:57:49.070 --> 00:57:51.950
The problem is that there were
few linguistic conventions

00:57:51.950 --> 00:57:55.500
for people to draw on for
formulating racialized desires

00:57:55.500 --> 00:57:57.740
outside of tropes of colorblindness,

00:57:57.740 --> 00:58:00.460
objectification, or exclusion.

00:58:00.460 --> 00:58:03.800
So some use the sexual
to find new grammars,

00:58:03.800 --> 00:58:07.890
some means of counter that
coalesce outside of language,

00:58:07.890 --> 00:58:10.640
ways of eroticizing in the body,

00:58:10.640 --> 00:58:14.880
what can be so difficult
to narrate interpersonally.

00:58:14.880 --> 00:58:18.690
So I really struggled with
deciding what anecdote

00:58:18.690 --> 00:58:21.190
from my field work to share
with you in the brief moments

00:58:21.190 --> 00:58:22.920
that I have for this provocation.

00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:26.050
And so I scrolled through my
Rolodex of favorite perverts,

00:58:26.050 --> 00:58:27.830
the upper east side,

00:58:27.830 --> 00:58:30.423
it's really, it's getting
bigger every minute,

00:58:31.590 --> 00:58:34.190
the upper east side
heterosexual Jewish couple

00:58:34.190 --> 00:58:37.410
who enjoyed Nazi play on Shabbat,

00:58:37.410 --> 00:58:40.450
the black-white lesbian
couple married for 20 years

00:58:40.450 --> 00:58:42.810
who engaged in practices
of objectification

00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:44.770
and the iconography of slavery.

00:58:44.770 --> 00:58:48.580
The norm core couple who
maintained a 1950s household.

00:58:48.580 --> 00:58:50.570
The American and Eastern European couple

00:58:50.570 --> 00:58:53.010
who did mail order bride play,

00:58:53.010 --> 00:58:55.040
or the time I was invited to participate

00:58:55.040 --> 00:58:56.330
in an interrogation scene

00:58:56.330 --> 00:58:58.940
by a well known black female dominant.

00:58:58.940 --> 00:59:02.890
All would've led me to kind
of the same set of conclusions

00:59:02.890 --> 00:59:06.290
that even in a context of
acute attunement to power,

00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:09.810
social differences produce
what Rachel Gorman calls

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:13.570
a quagmire of affect, a
hazardous terrain of inarticulate

00:59:13.570 --> 00:59:15.940
or invisible whiteness
that over-determines

00:59:15.940 --> 00:59:17.690
what we know how to think,

00:59:17.690 --> 00:59:19.440
what we know how we know to feel

00:59:19.440 --> 00:59:22.000
and what we are able to say.

00:59:22.000 --> 00:59:27.000
People of color who I interviewed
explicitly understood,

00:59:27.400 --> 00:59:31.150
and women as well, either
gendered or racialized play

00:59:31.150 --> 00:59:35.790
as either reformatting
objectification into recognition,

00:59:35.790 --> 00:59:37.970
reversing the terms of subjectification

00:59:37.970 --> 00:59:39.380
by either consenting to it

00:59:39.380 --> 00:59:42.270
and thus not fully being an object,

00:59:42.270 --> 00:59:44.620
but in fact, being a subject of it,

00:59:44.620 --> 00:59:47.930
repeating it to gain mastery
or spinning the iconography

00:59:47.930 --> 00:59:51.940
to subjectify either
maleness or whiteness.

00:59:51.940 --> 00:59:54.130
They tied it to emotional
states like fear,

00:59:54.130 --> 00:59:56.570
aggression, grief, shame, humiliation,

00:59:56.570 --> 00:59:59.920
but always in contexts
with a very specificity

00:59:59.920 --> 01:00:04.920
of their identities, and indeed,
BDSM identities themselves

01:00:04.930 --> 01:00:08.530
often merge sexual
practices with orientations

01:00:08.530 --> 01:00:11.890
to an individual's
particular constellation

01:00:11.890 --> 01:00:14.370
of gender, racial, and
sexual identification.

01:00:14.370 --> 01:00:15.991
So sociologists have written

01:00:15.991 --> 01:00:19.460
a lot about how the sort
of contemporary moment

01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:22.720
is a moment of specialized
erotics subcultures, right?

01:00:22.720 --> 01:00:24.700
And these erotic subcultures often trade

01:00:24.700 --> 01:00:27.540
not merely in what we want
to do with one another

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:28.780
or to one another,

01:00:28.780 --> 01:00:31.930
but in the very particularity
of our identities,

01:00:31.930 --> 01:00:35.460
and I mean that in a
deeply intersectional way.

01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:38.650
White subjects understood
or really offloaded

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:41.370
their racialized investments
onto their partners.

01:00:41.370 --> 01:00:44.330
I think of one white man who did race play

01:00:44.330 --> 01:00:45.517
with his black partner,

01:00:45.517 --> 01:00:47.970
"I am here to help her with
her process of reckoning,"

01:00:47.970 --> 01:00:52.410
he said, "with what was done
by my forefathers to hers."

01:00:52.410 --> 01:00:53.243
Right?

01:00:53.243 --> 01:00:57.810
So I'm wondering if I have
time to tell one quick story.

01:00:57.810 --> 01:00:58.840
I don't really, but I mean,

01:00:58.840 --> 01:01:01.683
there's so many stories
that I could tell in which,

01:01:03.410 --> 01:01:05.940
that reproduce this including ways

01:01:05.940 --> 01:01:09.600
in which I was recruited by subjects

01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:13.160
to take place in racialized scenes

01:01:13.160 --> 01:01:17.417
that forced me to kind of
confront my own inarticulateness

01:01:18.270 --> 01:01:20.140
about what it would
mean for me to do that.

01:01:20.140 --> 01:01:21.560
And I say this not to collapse

01:01:21.560 --> 01:01:23.520
into like an erotophobic discussion

01:01:23.520 --> 01:01:26.380
about why we should or shouldn't
touch research subjects.

01:01:26.380 --> 01:01:28.880
I have a whole paper on
that if you're interested.

01:01:30.050 --> 01:01:32.830
And I have a whole, yes, I
have a whole paper on that,

01:01:32.830 --> 01:01:35.640
but really like struggling
with the provocation made

01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:37.360
by Tiffany King and many others,

01:01:37.360 --> 01:01:39.660
for those of us working on
issues of social difference

01:01:39.660 --> 01:01:40.760
from the position of whiteness

01:01:40.760 --> 01:01:43.520
to really examine our
investments in racialization

01:01:43.520 --> 01:01:45.310
or in avoiding its capture.

01:01:45.310 --> 01:01:47.660
And I was thinking when
Che was talking earlier

01:01:49.032 --> 01:01:53.840
about political experiments
beyond restitution,

01:01:53.840 --> 01:01:57.640
that the interpersonal
domain in a context of trust,

01:01:57.640 --> 01:02:01.390
in a context of mutuality
is a place where many people

01:02:01.390 --> 01:02:04.330
are doing some of these experiments

01:02:04.330 --> 01:02:07.703
in more just means of
attachment to one another.

01:02:08.880 --> 01:02:12.230
And I think that I kind of
wanna argue for an agenda

01:02:12.230 --> 01:02:17.230
that foregrounds what issues
of justice require of us

01:02:18.080 --> 01:02:21.430
in our most intimate
encounters with others, right?

01:02:21.430 --> 01:02:24.550
And to confront how we're going
to confront these affective

01:02:24.550 --> 01:02:27.073
and linguistic failures that so many of us

01:02:27.073 --> 01:02:29.070
were enculturated into.

01:02:29.070 --> 01:02:32.500
But like most things that
are worth deep consideration,

01:02:32.500 --> 01:02:33.960
this is not a new idea.

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:37.960
In fact, Robert Reed far
articulated this in 1998

01:02:37.960 --> 01:02:41.350
when he wrote quote, and
I'm gonna end on this idea,

01:02:41.350 --> 01:02:44.530
what is difficult to accept is
the idea that the sexual act,

01:02:44.530 --> 01:02:47.220
at least as it is performed by queers,

01:02:47.220 --> 01:02:51.020
it's not necessarily a good
expansive or liberatory thing.

01:02:51.020 --> 01:02:53.230
A place in which individuals
exist for a moment

01:02:53.230 --> 01:02:56.060
outside of themselves such
that new possibilities

01:02:56.060 --> 01:02:58.510
are at once imagined and articulated.

01:02:58.510 --> 01:03:01.320
Indeed I'm suggesting that this notion

01:03:01.320 --> 01:03:03.950
is itself predicated upon the articulation

01:03:03.950 --> 01:03:05.700
of a set of false boundaries.

01:03:05.700 --> 01:03:08.840
We do not escape race
and racism when we f,

01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:12.430
on the contrary, this fantasy
of escape is precisely

01:03:12.430 --> 01:03:16.080
that which marks the sexual
act as deeply implicated

01:03:16.080 --> 01:03:18.070
in the ideological processes,

01:03:18.070 --> 01:03:21.100
by which difference is
constructed and maintained.

01:03:21.100 --> 01:03:22.640
The task that awaits all of us then

01:03:22.640 --> 01:03:24.540
is to speak desire plainly,

01:03:24.540 --> 01:03:27.140
to pay attention to what we think when f,

01:03:27.140 --> 01:03:29.990
more, it is the particular
task of white men

01:03:29.990 --> 01:03:32.120
to give up the comforts of naivete

01:03:32.120 --> 01:03:34.480
of banal gestures to racial inclusion.

01:03:34.480 --> 01:03:36.640
Indeed, the work before us is precisely

01:03:36.640 --> 01:03:38.740
to put our own bodies on the line.

01:03:38.740 --> 01:03:41.520
We must refuse to allow
production of a queer theory,

01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:44.700
so reified that it does
nothing to challenge

01:03:44.700 --> 01:03:48.510
the way we interact, the way
we think, and the way we f.

01:03:48.510 --> 01:03:50.580
We must not only think as we f,

01:03:50.580 --> 01:03:53.900
but also pay close attention
to all the implications,

01:03:53.900 --> 01:03:57.510
good and bad, of those
sometimes startling thoughts.

01:03:57.510 --> 01:03:58.588
Thank you.

01:03:58.588 --> 01:04:01.755
(audience applauding)

01:04:10.391 --> 01:04:13.640
- So, oh, it is on, thank you all so much.

01:04:13.640 --> 01:04:15.200
That was terrific.

01:04:15.200 --> 01:04:19.070
I think we could just
move to your questions.

01:04:19.070 --> 01:04:20.300
I don't think there's a mic there,

01:04:20.300 --> 01:04:22.570
so maybe I'll run around
on with the mic, does that?

01:04:22.570 --> 01:04:24.210
Yeah, okay.

01:04:24.210 --> 01:04:25.720
But I think I'll turn it to you all

01:04:25.720 --> 01:04:27.370
if there's that awkward
silence, I'll go first,

01:04:27.370 --> 01:04:28.500
but is that a question?

01:04:28.500 --> 01:04:30.320
Yeah, amazing, here.

01:04:30.320 --> 01:04:33.192
- Yeah, thank you all so much
coming to speak with us today,

01:04:33.192 --> 01:04:34.968
and thank you so much,
Joe, for organizing this.

01:04:34.968 --> 01:04:36.354
I have question regarding refactoring

01:04:36.354 --> 01:04:38.670
I always wanted to address it.

01:04:38.670 --> 01:04:41.010
I'm interested in kind of other ways

01:04:41.010 --> 01:04:42.870
in which, okay, totally.

01:04:42.870 --> 01:04:43.703
Thank you.

01:04:46.940 --> 01:04:49.910
I know you were talking
about kind of how to disrupt

01:04:49.910 --> 01:04:51.300
the violence that occurs within the home

01:04:51.300 --> 01:04:53.430
by like bringing grief into public space.

01:04:53.430 --> 01:04:55.010
I'm interested if you or if anyone else

01:04:55.010 --> 01:04:58.060
has other like thoughts on how to, yeah,

01:04:58.060 --> 01:05:00.520
on how to disrupt like just
the interpersonal violence

01:05:00.520 --> 01:05:01.370
that occurs within the home

01:05:01.370 --> 01:05:04.140
and like what are ways to
bring like women who have sex

01:05:04.140 --> 01:05:06.350
or like women who are
murdered because they have sex

01:05:06.350 --> 01:05:09.390
into public space and like
what are the ideas you have

01:05:09.390 --> 01:05:10.553
about that or thoughts.

01:05:14.980 --> 01:05:16.693
- Do you want me to answer.
- Yeah, go for it.

01:05:23.520 --> 01:05:25.210
- I don't know why I did that.

01:05:25.210 --> 01:05:26.390
No, so this is something

01:05:26.390 --> 01:05:28.380
that I'm really still thinking about.

01:05:28.380 --> 01:05:31.430
This is, I'm what, three fifths of the way

01:05:31.430 --> 01:05:32.633
through the book now.

01:05:34.130 --> 01:05:35.133
So,

01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:40.440
I'm thinking about
community accountability

01:05:40.440 --> 01:05:45.440
and about what our own,

01:05:45.520 --> 01:05:47.400
what our collective responsibility

01:05:47.400 --> 01:05:51.190
towards interrupting violence.

01:05:51.190 --> 01:05:54.870
I think Joe posed the
question about aesthetics.

01:05:54.870 --> 01:05:56.137
I think that has an important role,

01:05:56.137 --> 01:05:57.750
and my students have talked

01:05:59.910 --> 01:06:04.318
in really convincing ways
about what the role of art is

01:06:04.318 --> 01:06:06.600
in interrupting rape culture.

01:06:06.600 --> 01:06:11.600
And students are most comfortable
thinking that education

01:06:11.900 --> 01:06:14.060
is the way in which we have to do,

01:06:14.060 --> 01:06:15.463
in which we access

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:22.950
and put into practice our
collective responsibilities,

01:06:22.950 --> 01:06:25.170
but it is something that
I'm still thinking through,

01:06:25.170 --> 01:06:27.680
and I actually would
welcome if other panelists

01:06:27.680 --> 01:06:29.080
have something to say about it,

01:06:29.080 --> 01:06:34.080
but I am early in thinking
about what this means.

01:06:34.890 --> 01:06:39.890
I do, yeah, I'll just say that,

01:06:40.050 --> 01:06:42.810
that I know that it's about

01:06:44.800 --> 01:06:46.910
a line between publicity
and privacy, right?

01:06:46.910 --> 01:06:49.720
So I mean, playing on
the public private divide

01:06:49.720 --> 01:06:52.950
and specifically in intersectional
public private divide

01:06:52.950 --> 01:06:55.640
where black women have
never belonged in private

01:06:55.640 --> 01:06:56.950
in the same way that black men

01:06:56.950 --> 01:07:00.280
kind of don't belong in public
space and their decision

01:07:01.880 --> 01:07:03.650
to bring the grief into the public

01:07:03.650 --> 01:07:06.770
when they didn't have
any real private, right?

01:07:06.770 --> 01:07:09.080
Is an important one.

01:07:09.080 --> 01:07:11.640
And trying to figure out the ways in which

01:07:11.640 --> 01:07:14.460
to make these forms of
violence more visible

01:07:14.460 --> 01:07:17.380
is the thing that brought
me to this project, right?

01:07:17.380 --> 01:07:22.147
And so the fact that Barfield
is thinking constantly

01:07:23.450 --> 01:07:26.590
about memorialization, about
statues, about artwork,

01:07:26.590 --> 01:07:29.640
about making people into
more than statistics

01:07:29.640 --> 01:07:30.880
into making that visible,

01:07:30.880 --> 01:07:33.170
I think has something very,

01:07:33.170 --> 01:07:35.070
like is an important part of this,

01:07:35.070 --> 01:07:38.400
but it's about, I could
go off on a whole tangent

01:07:38.400 --> 01:07:40.970
about how Les Moonves was at CBSs

01:07:40.970 --> 01:07:42.970
and canceled "Designing
Women" and this like,

01:07:42.970 --> 01:07:46.820
and then put a generation of
murdered women on TV, right?

01:07:46.820 --> 01:07:48.700
And put that into our visual culture

01:07:48.700 --> 01:07:52.440
and what it means that Tarana
Burke has a movie deal,

01:07:52.440 --> 01:07:55.290
and how I think that that is
important for thinking about

01:07:56.160 --> 01:07:57.870
how we interrupt those kinds of things.

01:07:57.870 --> 01:08:00.260
So that's my, not quite there yet,

01:08:00.260 --> 01:08:02.710
but the things that are moving
around in my head about it.

01:08:02.710 --> 01:08:03.683
Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:08:05.140 --> 01:08:07.180
- I guess this is on.
- Yeah.

01:08:07.180 --> 01:08:09.520
- Yeah, I just wanted to,

01:08:09.520 --> 01:08:11.140
I appreciate the question so much

01:08:11.140 --> 01:08:14.913
and your presentation
so much, and I really,

01:08:16.050 --> 01:08:17.750
it made me think a lot
about visual justice,

01:08:17.750 --> 01:08:20.510
especially like the Ida B. Wells image

01:08:20.510 --> 01:08:22.810
where she inserts herself in the picture

01:08:22.810 --> 01:08:24.100
and then brings everybody

01:08:24.100 --> 01:08:26.190
who's not in the picture into the fold

01:08:26.190 --> 01:08:27.973
and that was so moving.

01:08:30.010 --> 01:08:31.830
And I also, I think the question

01:08:31.830 --> 01:08:33.430
in your presentation makes me think

01:08:33.430 --> 01:08:35.570
about black trans woman being killed

01:08:35.570 --> 01:08:40.540
both in private and in public
and in spectacular ways,

01:08:40.540 --> 01:08:44.210
but despite the kind of spectacle,

01:08:44.210 --> 01:08:47.173
horrifying spectacle of that violence,

01:08:48.350 --> 01:08:51.513
there's not the political
mobilization as a response to it.

01:08:52.390 --> 01:08:56.480
And so it just made me think about that

01:08:56.480 --> 01:09:00.600
until really recently, like,
I think it was last summer,

01:09:00.600 --> 01:09:05.270
I went to a black trans
pride march in Brooklyn,

01:09:05.270 --> 01:09:07.760
outside of the Brooklyn Museum.

01:09:07.760 --> 01:09:10.633
And there were like 10 to 15,000 people.

01:09:11.750 --> 01:09:14.313
And that was unprecedented,

01:09:15.420 --> 01:09:18.633
which felt like such a moving event,

01:09:19.960 --> 01:09:24.120
but like how to sustain that momentum

01:09:25.500 --> 01:09:26.993
in between the marches,

01:09:28.556 --> 01:09:30.956
it's still like an ongoing,
like how to do that,

01:09:32.330 --> 01:09:33.980
like beyond visibility, basically.

01:09:33.980 --> 01:09:34.923
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:09:36.496 --> 01:09:37.740
- [Joe] Should I hold up?

01:09:37.740 --> 01:09:40.290
- Yeah, I mean, I wrote this down

01:09:40.290 --> 01:09:44.180
because I think that there's
so much to think about

01:09:45.480 --> 01:09:46.550
in captive genders

01:09:46.550 --> 01:09:49.020
where they talk about the
phenomenon of overkill,

01:09:49.020 --> 01:09:51.760
but no one writes about it, right?

01:09:51.760 --> 01:09:55.730
So we know that we only
find out about black women

01:09:55.730 --> 01:09:58.050
being killed when they're
killed in the same news cycle

01:09:58.050 --> 01:10:00.490
or by the same departments
that kill black men, right?

01:10:00.490 --> 01:10:04.860
And so it follows that it
would be worse in a situation

01:10:04.860 --> 01:10:06.640
where there's a suppression of information

01:10:06.640 --> 01:10:09.230
and are not gonna talk about
it for black trans women

01:10:09.230 --> 01:10:11.350
that we just, we won't know,

01:10:11.350 --> 01:10:13.420
so we don't talk about it, right?

01:10:13.420 --> 01:10:15.860
But the moving in the
public private divide

01:10:15.860 --> 01:10:18.270
is really, it's tremendous.

01:10:18.270 --> 01:10:22.327
But I do think that, as with Stonewall,

01:10:25.440 --> 01:10:28.930
as with Save Our Sons
and Daughters, right?

01:10:28.930 --> 01:10:32.600
As with Antifa, right,

01:10:32.600 --> 01:10:34.720
never let a Nazi hold the street, right?

01:10:34.720 --> 01:10:36.920
Being in the public,

01:10:36.920 --> 01:10:39.180
and decision to occupy a public space

01:10:39.180 --> 01:10:41.120
is a part of what this is, right?

01:10:41.120 --> 01:10:46.120
And being there, being out
is a part of the solution.

01:10:46.120 --> 01:10:50.080
And so even if it can't ever be 10,000

01:10:50.080 --> 01:10:53.760
or there are only gonna be sporadic times

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:55.010
when it's gonna be that many,

01:10:55.010 --> 01:11:00.010
like it is thinking about the
other ways to keep us there

01:11:00.430 --> 01:11:03.337
is the thing that we're
fighting for, right?

01:11:06.210 --> 01:11:07.570
- [Joe] Yeah. Go for it.

01:11:08.987 --> 01:11:10.230
- Thanks, Jeff.

01:11:10.230 --> 01:11:14.940
Hi, I'm Olivia, thanks
so much for your talk.

01:11:14.940 --> 01:11:17.733
So listening to all of you
speak has me thinking about,

01:11:18.662 --> 01:11:19.750
and this question has me thinking

01:11:19.750 --> 01:11:22.610
about M. Jacqui Alexander's
idea of the palimpsest, right?

01:11:22.610 --> 01:11:25.520
Which would have us think that
colonial forms of violence

01:11:25.520 --> 01:11:28.810
have an arrangement of
like here and there,

01:11:28.810 --> 01:11:31.860
and then and now, never
the twain shall meet.

01:11:31.860 --> 01:11:34.640
And it seems like of you are
kind of invoking the importance

01:11:34.640 --> 01:11:37.200
of at least some kind of
palimpsestic way of thinking

01:11:37.200 --> 01:11:42.200
of we're thinking about here
and now, and then and there,

01:11:44.520 --> 01:11:48.290
because I teach many of your works,

01:11:48.290 --> 01:11:51.410
I'm wondering if you guys can talk about

01:11:51.410 --> 01:11:53.510
the kind of pedagogical implications

01:11:53.510 --> 01:11:55.930
of this palimpsestic way of thinking

01:11:55.930 --> 01:11:58.230
or however you choose to interpret the way

01:11:58.230 --> 01:12:01.660
that you are all thinking
about historical contingencies

01:12:01.660 --> 01:12:03.690
here and there, then and now

01:12:04.630 --> 01:12:07.250
for actually kind of bringing about

01:12:07.250 --> 01:12:10.573
the various forms of sexual
justice you're invested in.

01:12:11.470 --> 01:12:13.240
So I guess a short version of the question

01:12:13.240 --> 01:12:16.730
is how does thinking about
the sort of deep histories

01:12:16.730 --> 01:12:17.983
in all of your work,

01:12:18.940 --> 01:12:22.560
how does that play a key role
in sort of thinking about

01:12:22.560 --> 01:12:26.223
and manifesting an idea of sexual justice?

01:12:31.690 --> 01:12:33.570
- You got, I'm gonna take
the liberty of a follow up,

01:12:33.570 --> 01:12:35.240
which is, there's a strain,

01:12:35.240 --> 01:12:38.260
like I said, "Eve" Cedrician strain

01:12:38.260 --> 01:12:40.480
of the emphasis on the reparative

01:12:40.480 --> 01:12:41.877
and not to get caught
in the paranoid, right?

01:12:41.877 --> 01:12:43.650
And the kinds of ways that
the paranoid can be circular.

01:12:43.650 --> 01:12:45.530
And so I want you to answer that question.

01:12:45.530 --> 01:12:48.630
I mean, but to think, how can
you think the palimpsestic

01:12:48.630 --> 01:12:50.280
along with,

01:12:50.280 --> 01:12:51.210
how do you think the palimpsestic

01:12:51.210 --> 01:12:54.020
and also the reparative at the same time,

01:12:54.020 --> 01:12:55.450
I'll leave it there, right?

01:12:55.450 --> 01:12:56.450
Or is the palimpsestic,

01:12:56.450 --> 01:12:58.310
is it always kind of a paranoid turn?

01:12:58.310 --> 01:13:01.220
Does it have to be, do
you see where I'm going?

01:13:01.220 --> 01:13:03.283
Okay, go with Olivia first, yeah.

01:13:07.390 --> 01:13:10.940
- Well, I mean, is the word palimpsest,

01:13:10.940 --> 01:13:13.674
I'm so bad at English, but anyways.

01:13:13.674 --> 01:13:15.693
- Oh yeah, it's just a phenomenal era,

01:13:15.693 --> 01:13:17.820
it's something--
- You write over and over.

01:13:17.820 --> 01:13:18.731
- Right, right.
- Yeah.

01:13:18.731 --> 01:13:19.564
I think between the mask

01:13:19.564 --> 01:13:21.350
and my like fear of my
terrible vocabulary,

01:13:21.350 --> 01:13:22.660
I was like, is that the word?

01:13:22.660 --> 01:13:24.870
I think for me, in terms of pedagogically,

01:13:24.870 --> 01:13:27.340
like I teach at Brooklyn college

01:13:27.340 --> 01:13:30.010
and like the last few semesters,

01:13:30.010 --> 01:13:33.050
I've been almost the only
white person in the room.

01:13:33.050 --> 01:13:37.410
So I feel like my role in this situation

01:13:37.410 --> 01:13:40.450
can be in classes of like
intro to political theory.

01:13:40.450 --> 01:13:43.083
I can try to historicize
sexual injustices,

01:13:45.060 --> 01:13:47.080
a lot of those folks, and
maybe they need details,

01:13:47.080 --> 01:13:49.740
but they don't need my help
historicizing questions

01:13:49.740 --> 01:13:52.200
of like structural inequality
or histories of racism.

01:13:52.200 --> 01:13:55.940
So it can be like a, in terms
of the mutual conversation

01:13:55.940 --> 01:13:56.827
between professor and students,

01:13:56.827 --> 01:13:58.950
and are at different
locations of privilege,

01:13:58.950 --> 01:14:01.260
it could be like really generative.

01:14:01.260 --> 01:14:05.260
But I feel like I learn
more from being in a room

01:14:05.260 --> 01:14:06.670
with people who are living

01:14:06.670 --> 01:14:09.038
in this never ending colonial moment

01:14:09.038 --> 01:14:11.130
in terms of that informing my own work

01:14:11.130 --> 01:14:13.793
on what we're calling
today sexual justice.

01:14:17.280 --> 01:14:20.270
- Yeah, I mean, I resonate with that idea

01:14:20.270 --> 01:14:23.520
in the sense that part of the intervention

01:14:23.520 --> 01:14:26.532
that I think I was trying
to make is that the idea

01:14:26.532 --> 01:14:30.560
that the current moment isn't always

01:14:30.560 --> 01:14:35.530
an overwriting of a past is
really only a perspective one

01:14:35.530 --> 01:14:38.540
can hold from a position of
incredible privilege, right?

01:14:38.540 --> 01:14:42.487
And so there's a way in
which I think students come

01:14:44.670 --> 01:14:48.350
into classrooms with an idea of equality

01:14:48.350 --> 01:14:53.070
that is based in identity for people

01:14:53.070 --> 01:14:58.070
who have more of a tangential
relationship to privilege,

01:14:59.070 --> 01:15:04.070
and based more in a sense
of sameness for people

01:15:05.010 --> 01:15:06.570
who have a tremendous degree of privilege,

01:15:06.570 --> 01:15:08.940
whether that's economic
privilege, gender privilege,

01:15:08.940 --> 01:15:11.610
racial privilege, whatever you wanna say.

01:15:11.610 --> 01:15:13.243
And so I think that,

01:15:15.580 --> 01:15:18.490
I mean, pedagogy has to meet people

01:15:18.490 --> 01:15:23.490
where they enter a classroom
and move them one step beyond

01:15:23.500 --> 01:15:24.730
where they came in, right?

01:15:24.730 --> 01:15:25.820
Like that's what I consider

01:15:25.820 --> 01:15:28.810
to be a successful a class, right?

01:15:28.810 --> 01:15:30.670
Is that I've taken
somebody one step beyond

01:15:30.670 --> 01:15:31.503
their starting point,

01:15:31.503 --> 01:15:34.600
and people I'm from very
different starting points, right?

01:15:34.600 --> 01:15:37.700
And so I think on some level,

01:15:37.700 --> 01:15:40.330
what we have to explain and
to whom as Paisley pointed out

01:15:40.330 --> 01:15:43.823
is really location dependent.

01:15:54.493 --> 01:15:57.880
- So I've participated in a
seminar on the uses of the past.

01:15:57.880 --> 01:16:01.660
And part of what came out
of that for my thinking was,

01:16:01.660 --> 01:16:05.980
and it's in the book talking
about the power that lynching

01:16:05.980 --> 01:16:10.030
has on contemporary US black politics,

01:16:10.030 --> 01:16:13.820
and about the ways in
which lynching becomes away

01:16:13.820 --> 01:16:14.947
that we talk about things

01:16:14.947 --> 01:16:19.947
and the significance of
that for black women.

01:16:20.080 --> 01:16:23.610
So very few black women
relatively were lynched,

01:16:23.610 --> 01:16:27.193
about 5% according to your crystal fester.

01:16:28.574 --> 01:16:32.410
And many of them were lynched
in place of a husband,

01:16:32.410 --> 01:16:34.510
father, a brother that they couldn't find.

01:16:35.998 --> 01:16:40.120
And so this means a lot
and this is significant.

01:16:40.120 --> 01:16:44.510
And I think beyond that,
to talk about palimpsest,

01:16:44.510 --> 01:16:48.010
it's the work that Du
Bois as the best among

01:16:48.010 --> 01:16:50.810
but many others did

01:16:50.810 --> 01:16:53.790
to connect lynching
and crucifixion, right?

01:16:53.790 --> 01:16:57.290
So Du Bois has five, six lynching tales

01:16:57.290 --> 01:16:59.790
where he brings a black Jesus to life

01:16:59.790 --> 01:17:01.540
in the Jim Crow South.

01:17:01.540 --> 01:17:03.950
He only speaks the words of
the biblical Jesus Christ,

01:17:03.950 --> 01:17:07.323
this indicts everything that
goes on and he dies for it.

01:17:08.833 --> 01:17:12.040
And I think that the martyr themes, right,

01:17:12.040 --> 01:17:13.270
the resurrective work,

01:17:13.270 --> 01:17:17.250
the things that are going
on are a powerful story.

01:17:17.250 --> 01:17:21.160
And I've said, like a former
person here, Roger Smith,

01:17:21.160 --> 01:17:22.460
who's not here anymore,

01:17:22.460 --> 01:17:24.990
it's important in the
story of black people-hood,

01:17:24.990 --> 01:17:27.570
and part of what black
women are gonna have to do

01:17:28.930 --> 01:17:30.860
is tell a new story, right?

01:17:30.860 --> 01:17:33.693
'Cause this is a rewriting
over and over again.

01:17:35.440 --> 01:17:39.820
What do we do that black men
took the climax of the Bible,

01:17:39.820 --> 01:17:42.720
but luckily we have Saidiya
Hartman and Toni Morrison

01:17:42.720 --> 01:17:44.930
and others from whom
that we can think about

01:17:44.930 --> 01:17:47.770
how to deal with these kinds of deaths

01:17:47.770 --> 01:17:49.910
and ungrieved deaths.

01:17:49.910 --> 01:17:53.490
But that's gonna be the way
that to write a new story,

01:17:53.490 --> 01:17:55.170
to stop writing over the past

01:17:55.170 --> 01:18:00.170
and think about how to put
one at least on equal footing

01:18:01.200 --> 01:18:04.593
for thinking about the
origins of our politics.

01:18:06.580 --> 01:18:08.590
- Thank you so much for that question,

01:18:08.590 --> 01:18:13.590
and I felt really like transformed,

01:18:14.870 --> 01:18:17.170
I guess I would say by reading
"Pedagogies of Crossing",

01:18:17.170 --> 01:18:22.170
and so I always feel like
Jacqui Alexander's work

01:18:24.610 --> 01:18:26.750
is like a condition of
possibility for my own thinking

01:18:26.750 --> 01:18:29.950
and I feel very indebted to her labors,

01:18:29.950 --> 01:18:31.700
critical labors, conceptual labors.

01:18:32.700 --> 01:18:36.380
And I guess your question makes me think

01:18:36.380 --> 01:18:40.810
about how to have an
abolitionist pedagogy,

01:18:40.810 --> 01:18:43.480
both inside the university classroom

01:18:43.480 --> 01:18:48.260
and what that might mean as
a worker in the university

01:18:49.560 --> 01:18:53.390
and also inside the space of the prison.

01:18:53.390 --> 01:18:58.030
And like what does an
abolitionist pedagogy look like

01:18:58.030 --> 01:19:00.823
when you're teaching inside and outside?

01:19:01.880 --> 01:19:06.520
And it also, I showed some of,

01:19:06.520 --> 01:19:09.593
well, an image from
Tourmaline's film "Salacia",

01:19:09.593 --> 01:19:13.690
and she really like folds
time and folds space.

01:19:13.690 --> 01:19:18.140
And I think part of what that
does is like show the ruse

01:19:18.140 --> 01:19:23.140
of racial liberal progress
narratives that would tell us

01:19:23.210 --> 01:19:27.260
that for example cross-dressing
is decriminalized,

01:19:27.260 --> 01:19:28.770
when it's not in prisons, right?

01:19:28.770 --> 01:19:33.770
So I think that like showing
the foil of this teleological

01:19:36.900 --> 01:19:41.340
kind of racial liberal time zone

01:19:41.340 --> 01:19:44.593
is like part of what palimpsest,

01:19:44.593 --> 01:19:46.620
now I'm like mispronounce it.
- Palimpsest.

01:19:46.620 --> 01:19:48.803
- Part of what that project does,

01:19:49.870 --> 01:19:51.800
and I think that that is crucial

01:19:51.800 --> 01:19:54.800
to part of the demystification work

01:19:54.800 --> 01:19:58.300
that an abolitionist pedagogy can offer,

01:19:58.300 --> 01:19:59.133
can and does offer.

01:19:59.133 --> 01:20:01.420
So thanks.
- Thank you.

01:20:01.420 --> 01:20:04.430
- [Joe] I think maybe one more question.

01:20:04.430 --> 01:20:05.620
I feel like it's close to seven.

01:20:05.620 --> 01:20:06.453
Okay, great.

01:20:09.860 --> 01:20:11.960
- [Gabby] Yeah, hi there, oh, that's loud.

01:20:13.116 --> 01:20:16.747
I'm Gabby, Thank you so much
for coming to speak with us.

01:20:16.747 --> 01:20:19.290
As you were all talking,

01:20:19.290 --> 01:20:22.360
I was thinking a lot
about sexual injustice

01:20:22.360 --> 01:20:25.830
beyond questions of consent,

01:20:25.830 --> 01:20:29.540
like the unjust formation
of one's sexual proclivities

01:20:29.540 --> 01:20:32.700
and preferences, sexual racism, et cetera.

01:20:32.700 --> 01:20:36.970
And I am curious to hear from any of you

01:20:36.970 --> 01:20:38.740
how we should approach talking

01:20:38.740 --> 01:20:41.590
about how desire has a politics

01:20:41.590 --> 01:20:43.930
and talking about ways to individually

01:20:43.930 --> 01:20:46.930
and collectively reckon with that politics

01:20:46.930 --> 01:20:51.930
without sliding into what sort
of sex positivity advocates

01:20:52.890 --> 01:20:57.203
would call like prudish or
authoritarian sexual moralizing.

01:20:59.358 --> 01:21:02.868
- I think that's on you.
- But you're gonna start.

01:21:02.868 --> 01:21:04.283
You don't wanna take that one on?

01:21:05.680 --> 01:21:07.673
I mean, look,

01:21:10.569 --> 01:21:11.980
there are a lot of sociologists right now

01:21:11.980 --> 01:21:14.320
that have been working
on sexual racism, right?

01:21:14.320 --> 01:21:17.960
And I think that there are
these sort of two formulations.

01:21:17.960 --> 01:21:22.960
One is that the intimate
realm is a place where,

01:21:26.370 --> 01:21:28.210
it functions as a marketplace, right?

01:21:28.210 --> 01:21:31.670
So that's often how sociologists
think about it, right?

01:21:31.670 --> 01:21:35.210
That there is something
called a sexual field

01:21:35.210 --> 01:21:38.240
in a Bourdieusian sense
in which different people,

01:21:38.240 --> 01:21:41.480
because of what they look
like and their social position

01:21:41.480 --> 01:21:43.050
have different amounts of erotic capital.

01:21:43.050 --> 01:21:46.310
And this functions in
very predictable ways

01:21:46.310 --> 01:21:49.240
to disadvantage women, people of color,

01:21:49.240 --> 01:21:51.750
people who don't fit sort
of hegemonic standards

01:21:51.750 --> 01:21:53.230
of beauty and so forth.

01:21:53.230 --> 01:21:57.710
And that that is the
root from which we need

01:21:57.710 --> 01:21:59.390
to begin to politicize desire.

01:21:59.390 --> 01:22:01.430
Like what does it mean to,

01:22:01.430 --> 01:22:04.550
I mean, it's sort of like,

01:22:04.550 --> 01:22:07.680
there's like a website called
Douchebags of Grindr, right?

01:22:07.680 --> 01:22:09.400
For example, where it's just like,

01:22:09.400 --> 01:22:11.530
basically like all the
men's profiles who are like

01:22:11.530 --> 01:22:14.210
no fats, no fems, no
blacks, like those people,

01:22:14.210 --> 01:22:19.210
and as if they are an
individual level problem, right?

01:22:21.360 --> 01:22:26.360
Whereas the actual problem
of desire is so much deeper

01:22:29.640 --> 01:22:34.640
than bad behavior done
publicly by one person, right?

01:22:35.800 --> 01:22:38.930
We are all complicit
in structures of desire

01:22:38.930 --> 01:22:41.053
that disadvantage,

01:22:41.053 --> 01:22:42.112
it's kind of like

01:22:42.112 --> 01:22:45.500
when my mother wants to
give my daughter lessons

01:22:45.500 --> 01:22:48.630
about sexuality, which I really
never want her to do ever,

01:22:48.630 --> 01:22:51.500
and she's just like, "Date the nice boy."

01:22:51.500 --> 01:22:54.780
But the truth is she means
like the nice handsome boy

01:22:54.780 --> 01:22:55.790
who's gonna be a doctor, right?

01:22:55.790 --> 01:22:57.710
Like that's what she means.

01:22:57.710 --> 01:22:59.920
My daughter has come out as straight,

01:22:59.920 --> 01:23:02.920
we're processing it in
therapy, I'm hoping.

01:23:02.920 --> 01:23:06.460
I'm hoping she'll get
over it at some point but,

01:23:06.460 --> 01:23:08.460
'cause heterosexuality these days seems,

01:23:08.460 --> 01:23:10.280
as Jane Ward says, fairly tragic.

01:23:10.280 --> 01:23:15.280
But another panel, but I do
think that the first step

01:23:18.600 --> 01:23:21.860
in having a different
conception of desire,

01:23:21.860 --> 01:23:25.830
and I hate this masculinist
economic framework

01:23:25.830 --> 01:23:27.130
for understanding desire,

01:23:27.130 --> 01:23:29.230
because I think that it's like everybody,

01:23:29.230 --> 01:23:31.270
some people have got a thicker wallet

01:23:31.270 --> 01:23:32.670
of desirability than others,

01:23:32.670 --> 01:23:37.610
and I just think that
desire is so much more

01:23:37.610 --> 01:23:41.550
of a, God, multivalent phenomenon.

01:23:41.550 --> 01:23:43.370
But I do think that we are all complicit

01:23:43.370 --> 01:23:46.010
in reproducing structures
of desire and desirability,

01:23:46.010 --> 01:23:48.630
both in our presentations of self,

01:23:48.630 --> 01:23:52.010
in the ways in which we value
or devalue other people,

01:23:52.010 --> 01:23:55.130
even in terms of like, I
hope he's a doctor, right?

01:23:55.130 --> 01:23:57.230
Like that is also a structure of desire.

01:23:57.230 --> 01:24:00.740
And it's one that doesn't
really account for,

01:24:00.740 --> 01:24:04.650
it's a cruel optimism, as
Lauren Berlant would say, right?

01:24:04.650 --> 01:24:07.330
That you imagine who the
perfect partner is gonna be,

01:24:07.330 --> 01:24:09.730
and oftentimes that
perfect partner is someone

01:24:09.730 --> 01:24:10.563
then you enter into

01:24:10.563 --> 01:24:12.220
like a completely miserable
marriage with, right?

01:24:12.220 --> 01:24:14.850
And so really thinking
about what are the things

01:24:14.850 --> 01:24:18.700
that make people desirable,
both erotically, right?

01:24:18.700 --> 01:24:19.533
And maybe it's something

01:24:19.533 --> 01:24:21.890
like they know what they're
doing in bed, right?

01:24:21.890 --> 01:24:25.960
That would be nice, I mean,
I'd like to see the app

01:24:25.960 --> 01:24:29.203
that kind of tries to get
at that algorithmically.

01:24:30.190 --> 01:24:32.963
If you know of it, my
email's available online.

01:24:34.290 --> 01:24:37.440
I mean, I think that part
of it is understanding

01:24:37.440 --> 01:24:41.980
our own investments in
particular forms of partnership,

01:24:41.980 --> 01:24:43.950
particular forms of erotic engagement

01:24:45.110 --> 01:24:49.633
as being part and parcel
of an overall kind of,

01:24:50.889 --> 01:24:51.722
like a structure of affect, right?

01:24:56.832 --> 01:24:59.415
- [Joe] Question from you guys?

01:25:01.022 --> 01:25:02.904
- We're good, okay?

01:25:02.904 --> 01:25:04.160
- Okay, I think, I think we're set.

01:25:04.160 --> 01:25:06.979
One last round of applause for our panel.

